he central 3x3\n  - star vs circle: radial extent (stars have long arms in 5 directions; circles have uniform radius ~2-3)\n\nFor a black star, the center of the star shape: for a filled star, the center is filled; for an open star, there's white in the center. Both stars have extent ~4-5 in the arm directions and ~2-3 in between arms.\n\nFor circles: extent ~2-3 in all directions. Open circle: white center.\n\nThis should be workable. In f93_detect.py I used the features area, whiten, bbox, maxrad, meanrad, fill, max/mean. Let me run that now and check the output.\n\nAlso about the legend: I need to exclude the legend region. The legend box seems to be roughly x from 55 to 230, y from 25 to 135. In previous scripts I excluded the region leg[:135,:210] and in_data x>73. But the legend markers are at x ~ 60-90, y from 30 to 125. Data starts at x ≈ 100 (300 K → pixel 100.8). Legend markers are at x ~ 65-95? Let me check: in the legend crop, the markers are at around x=85-110 in original coordinates (in the 4x crop, the marker circle is at x≈200-260 → original 50+200/4=100... hmm, wait, the crop was (40,0,260,120) resized 4x. The red circle in the crop is at about x=200-260 → original 40+50..65 = 90-105. Hmm, this is in the same x range as the data's leftmost point (100.8)!\n\nCareful. The legend markers are at original x ≈ 88-105, y ≈ 35-125 (from the 4x crop: red circle at y≈125-145 in the crop → original ~31-36; blue star at y≈190-215 → original ~48-54; open circle at y≈265-285 → original ~66-71; filled circle at y≈340-360 → original ~85-90; open star at y≈410-430 → original ~102-107; filled star at y≈475-495 → original ~119-124).\n\nSo the legend markers are at x ~ 90-105, y ~ 30-125. The leftmost data point (300 K, pixel 100.8) is at y values: from the image, at 300K the red circle is at zT≈0.2 → y pixel = 222.7 - 0.2*144.82 = 222.7-28.97 = 193.7. So the y positions of the leftmost data markers are roughly 180-215 (black series are lower: zT ~0.05-0.15 → y ~ 201-215). The legend's filled star is at y~120. So there's a y gap: legend markers are at y 30-125, leftmost data markers are at y ≥ ~175. Therefore, if I exclude the region y<160 and x<120, the legend can be safely excluded.\n\nBut careful: the upper-right data point (700K, zT=1.2-1.4) is at y ~ 22-52, x ~ 315-327 — that's fine, the legend is on the left side.\n\nAlso, in the legend text region, there are black characters (x > 110). The text is at x from ~125 to 230, y from 30 to 125. So the exclusion region should be: x < 240 AND y < 140 → exclude. In the data region, when x < 240, what's the minimum y of the data? At x=240 (T ≈ 547K), the red series' zT is about 0.9? y = 222.7-0.9*144.8 = 92. Uh oh! This is inside the exclusion region (x<240, y<140).\n\nHmm. Let me check: at 500K (pixel x = 100.8 + 200*0.565 = 213.8), red zT ~ 0.85 → y ≈ 100. So the red markers in the x range 200-240 are at y ~ 85-110, overlapping with the exclusion zone.\n\nI need to be more precise about the extent of the legend. Let me look for the borders of the legend box. From the full image, the legend box is: x from ~57 to ~232, y from ~28 to ~132. In the 4x legend crop (40,0,260,120): the box's left border is at crop x≈130 → original 40+32=72? Hmm, wait, looking at the legend crop image again: the box starts at around x≈130 in the 880-wide crop → 130/4=32.5 → original 40+32.5 = 72.5. Right border: not visible in the crop (does it continue past 260?). Actually, in the crop, the box extends past the right edge of the crop at 260. So the box goes at least to x=260 in the original coordinates. The text \"EuZn1.8Cd0.2Sb2\" ends around crop x=760 → original 40+190=230.\n\nThe bottom border of the box: from the original full image, the legend box's bottom is at y≈132 (below the YbZn2Sb2 row). In the 4x legend crop, the bottom border of the box is at crop y≈? The YbZn2Sb2 star is at crop y ~475-495 (original ~119-124), and the bottom border is at about y≈520 in the crop → original 130.\n\nSo the legend box: x 72-240, y 28-132. Let me verify by scanning for long horizontal/vertical black lines.\n\nStrategy: detect the legend box borders precisely, exclude everything inside it, and additionally handle the fact that the data at x<240, y<140... wait, can the data go inside the legend box? At pixel x=214 (T=500K), red zT≈0.85 → y≈100 — that's inside the legend box region (x<240, y<132)! Does the red curve actually pass through/under the legend box? Looking at the full image: at x≈200 (original) the red curve is at y≈... Let me look at the full image again. In the full image, the red curve at 500K (pixel ~214) is at zT~0.85-0.9. Y-axis: 0.9 → pixel 222.7-130 = 92. The legend box's bottom is ~132. So the red data point at x=214, y≈92 falls inside the legend box region x 72-240, y 28-132.\n\nHmm, wait, but in the actual image, does the legend box overlap with the red curve? Looking at the full image: the legend box is in the upper left. The red curve... at 500K, the red point appears to be just below the box, near its right edge. Let me look at the actual image more carefully. In the first view_image of the full figure: the legend box occupies the upper left, and the red curve rises from the lower left to the upper right, passing through the right side of the legend. At x≈200 the red curve is at about y≈95-100 (between the 0.8 and 1.0 gridlines). The legend box spans x≈72-240. So the red point at x≈214 (500K) is inside the box region x<240!\n\nLooking at the rendered full image: I see the red curve passing under/near the lower right corner of the legend box. In the 4x legend crop (40,0,260,120), in the lower right corner I can see red circles at around (650,540) in the crop, and a blue star below it at around (820,590) → original x≈202, y≈135, and x≈245, y≈148. Interesting! So at original (202,135) there's a red circle, and at (245,148) there's a blue star. And in the lower right of the crop I also see part of an open circle at (840, 555) → original (250, 139)?\n\nSo actually, the red/blue curve does pass through the lower part of the region below the legend box (y 130-160), in the x range 190-250. And the bottom border of the legend box is at y≈132. The red data point at x≈214 is at y≈135-140?? Hmm, this contradicts my zT estimate. Let me compute: if pixel (214, 135) → zT = (222.7-135)/144.82 = 87.7/144.82 = 0.606. And (245, 148) → blue zT = (222.7-148)/144.82 = 0.516.\n\nHmm, at 500K (pixel 213.8) I would expect the red zT ≈ 0.85 based on reading the full image, but... my reading from the full image might be off. Let me not guess; the data will tell me.\n\nOK, the point is this: the legend box borders are detectable as long straight lines. Data points are never at y<132 for x<240, unless they are inside the box. From the crop evidence, red data at x≈202-250 is at y≈135-150, which is below the bottom of the box (132). So it might just work to exclude x<245 AND y<133 (the interior of the legend box) — the red curve passes just below it. But it's tight. The red marker at (202,135): the radius is ~4, so it spans y 131-139 — touching the box border.\n\nThe safer approach: exclude the exact interior of the legend box (x 73-244, y 29-131) and let the marker detection handle the rest. The red marker at (202,135) is mostly below y=131, so most of its pixels survive. But my density-based detection might be weakened.\n\nAlternatively — a better approach: I know the series colors. Red and blue are easy: color mask, connected components, centroid. For black series, the legend markers are inside the box; if I exclude the box interior precisely, they're gone.\n\nLet me be systematic:\n\n1. Precisely find the legend box borders (detect long horizontal/vertical black lines in the upper-left quadrant).\n2. Red series: red mask, connected components (the red line + circles are all connected into one component — since the line connects the markers, the whole series is a single connected component). So instead: detect red circles by local density: convolve the red mask with a disk; the circles are thicker (diameter ~8-10 px) than the line (~2 px). Find local maxima above a threshold → centers.\n3. Blue series: same with the blue mask.\n4. Black series: all 3 black series + lines. Local density peaks. But circles on a line: the line adds density. Still, the local maximum at the marker center is higher. Then classify each peak as open/filled (central whiteness) and star/circle (radial extent / aspect).\n\nActually, the lines pass through the markers, so the density peak method works. The concern is that the line itself might create a spurious local maximum where it intersects the legend or the axis. Let me restrict to the data region and check.\n\nAlso, I need to assign black markers to the correct series (open circle vs filled circle vs open star vs filled star) and their x positions. The x positions should be the same across all 6 series (same temperatures). From the left crop, the marker spacing looks regular: let me count from the full image. The x-axis is 300→700, and the data markers are probably at 300, 310, 320, ..., 700 (41 points), or every 10 K (41 points), or every 20 K (21 points), or irregular (measurement temperatures).\n\nFrom the left crop (x from 85 to 200 original = 300 to about 505K... wait, pixel 85→T = 300 - (100.8-85)/0.565 ≈ 300-28 = 272? No wait: pixel 85 is to the left of the 300K tick (100.8). Data starts at pixel ~101 = 300K. In the left crop, the leftmost marker column is at crop x≈130 → original 85+130/8=85+16=101. Yes, that's 300K.\n\nMarker spacing in the left crop: the columns are at crop x ≈ 130, 220, 305, 395, 480, 565, 655, 745, 830... spacing ~85-90 px in the 8x crop → ~10.6-11.25 px in original → in K: 11/0.565 ≈ 19.5-19.8 K. So the data is every 20 K: 300, 320, 340, ..., 700 = 21 points.\n\nLet me verify: pixel for 320K = 100.8 + 20*0.565125 = 112.1; 340K → 123.4; ... 700K → 326.85. Spacing 11.3 px. In the left crop (8x): spacing ~90.6 px. Matches the ~85-90 observed.\n\nSo 21 points per series. Now let me run the detection.\n\nActually, from the summary I was getting 4 black markers per column on the right and 3 on the left. 4 black series = open circle (EuZn2Sb2), filled circle (EuCd2Sb2), open star (YbCd2Sb2), filled star (YbZn2Sb2). At low T, open circle and filled circle are close together, and open star and filled star are close together.\n\nHmm wait, actually looking at the left crop again, at 300K (leftmost): the rows from top to bottom are: red circle (topmost), blue star, then open circle + filled circle (close, the filled one below the open one), then open star + filled star (close), and... at the very bottom I see only one star row? The crop shows at 300K: red, blue, open circle, filled circle (just below the open one), open star, filled star. In the crop image, at the leftmost column: red circle at ~1250, blue star at ~1310, then a black open circle at ~1385, a black filled circle at ~1410 (just below), an open star at ~1455, and a filled star at ~1500. Yes, I can see 6 rows. Good.\n\nBut the summary said \"at the left, only 3 (open markers overlap / miss).\" So in the previous detection, at the leftmost column I was missing the open circle or the open star. Probably because at low T the open circle overlaps with the filled circle (zT is very close), and the open star overlaps with the filled star.\n\nLet me look at the zT values at 300K from the full image:\n- Red ≈ 0.22\n- Blue ≈ 0.20\n- Open circle (EuZn2Sb2) ≈ 0.15?\n- Filled circle (EuCd2Sb2) ≈ 0.13?\n- Open star (YbCd2Sb2) ≈ 0.10?\n- Filled star (YbZn2Sb2) ≈ 0.07?\n\nRoughly. At 300K these are all bunched between 0.05-0.22, which at 144.8 px per unit is 8-13 px apart. The marker size is ~8-10 px in diameter, so they really do overlap.\n\nOK, my plan: careful per-column detection using vertical black profiles at each expected x (21 positions), classifying each band. But overlapping markers in the same column... the bands will merge.\n\nA better alternative approach: