{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"reasoning","text":"Now I can clearly see the right edge:\n- Red filled circles (top)\n- Blue stars\n- Black open circles (two visible: one at about 660K, one at about 680K)\n- Black open stars (a series of about 4 stars in the middle: from low to high)\n- Black filled circles (2-3 visible on the left side of the crop)\n- Black filled stars (a series of 4 at the very bottom)\n\nSo the vertical ordering from top to bottom at high temperature: red circle > blue star > open circle > open star > filled circle > filled star?\n\nWait, looking at the crop: after the blue star, I see open circles at about 415-460 (two of them). Then open stars at about 520-570 (one at ~530, one at ~415 on the left side...). Hmm, let me re-read.\n\nFrom the crop (8x scale, origin at (280,20)):\n- Red circles: top-left area (about 40,330) and (about 130,300) → original coords (285,61), (296,57)\n- Blue stars: (about 20,365), (about 105,335), (about 190,265), (about 280,155) → original coords (282,66), (293,62), (304,53), (315,39)\n- Open circles: (about 15,415), (about 75,400) → original coords (282,72), (289,70)\n- Open star 1: (about 20,545)?? Actually I see an open star at the left edge (~10,548) → original (281,89)\n- Open star 2: (about 135,490) → original (297,81)\n- Open star 3: (about 195,415)?? Hmm no.\n\nLet me redo this carefully. What's visible in the right crop is:\n- Two open circles on the left: y_crop ≈ 415 and 400, x_crop ≈ 15 and 75 → original (282, 72) and (289, 70).\n- Open stars: one at x_crop≈10, y_crop≈548 → original (281,88); one at x_crop≈140, y_crop≈490 → original (297,81).\n\nHmm, wait, but between the open circles (y~70-72) and the open stars (y~81-88), at x=297 I see an open star at y=81, and at x=281 an open star at y=88. So the open-star series is below the open circles.\n\n- Filled circles: (about 30,620), (about 75,610), (about 140,585) → original (284,97.5), (289,96), (297,93).\n\nWait, that's odd — the filled circles are below the open stars? At x=297: open star at y=81, filled circle at y=93. Yes, filled circles are below the open stars.\n\n- Filled stars (bottom): (about 20,795), (about 95,770), (about 160,735), (about 245,670) → original (282,119), (292,116), (300,112), (311,104).\n\nSo at high T, the top-to-bottom ordering is:\n1. Red filled circles (EuZn1.8Cd0.2Sb2)\n2. Blue stars (YbCd1.6Zn0.4Sb2)\n3. Black open circles (EuZn2Sb2)\n4. Black open stars (YbCd2Sb2)\n5. Black filled circles (EuCd2Sb2)\n6. Black filled stars (YbZn2Sb2)\n\nInterestingly — at high T the filled circles are below the open stars, but at low T (left-side crop) the ordering is: red, blue, open circles, filled circles, open stars, filled stars? Let me re-check the left crop:\n\nFrom the left crop (8x, origin at (85,40)):\n- Red circles: (about 130,1245), (about 220,1215), (about 310,1170), (about 400,1120), (about 490,1080), (about 580,1020), (about 670,975), (about 760,930), (about 830,880), (about 900,810) → original coords x = 85+16.25=101.25, y=40+155.6=195.6; ...\n- Blue stars: below the red.\n- Open circles: (about 885,845) → original (295.6?? no wait. Hmm, 85+885/8 = 85+110.6 = 195.6, y = 40+105.6=145.6). Hmm, that's near the right edge of the left crop (crop x range is 0-115*8... no wait, the crop is (85,40,200,240) → width 115, height 200, resized 8x to 920x1600).\n\nSo the crop covers original x 85-200, y 40-240.\n\nThe rightmost open circle at crop (885, 845) → original (195.6, 145.6). And to its left: open circles at crop (790, 865)? → original (183.8, 148).\n\n- Filled circles: at crop x about 130: y_crop ≈ 1385 → original y = 40+173 = 213; open circles at the same x are at y_crop ≈ 1355?? Hmm, I need to be careful.\n\nActually, I can just trust the programmatic detection with classification. The key ambiguity (open vs filled, star vs circle) is resolvable by shape features. The tricky part is markers that overlap or are close together.\n\nSo, let me write a comprehensive detection script:\n\n1. Load the image, build masks:\n   - red: R>150, G<110, B<110\n   - blue: B>150, R<110, G<110\n   - black: R<110 & G<110 & B<110\n   - white: R>170 & G>170 & B>170\n\n2. Detect the legend box: find long horizontal/vertical black lines in the upper-left region. Or hardcode from visual inspection: the box spans roughly x 72-244, y 28-132. Detect precisely:\n   - For each row y in 0..150: count the number of black pixels for x in 60..260; box border rows have a long run.\n   - For each column x in 50..270: count the number of black pixels for y in 20..150.\n\n3. Marker detection:\n   - For red: convolve the red mask with a 9x9 ones kernel; local maxima above a threshold → centers.\n   - For blue: same.\n   - For black: convolve the black mask (excluding the legend box interior, axis lines, and tick marks) with a 9x9 ones kernel; local maxima above a threshold.\n\n4. Classify black markers using features (central whiteness, radial extent, maxrad/meanrad ratio).\n\n5. Snap x positions to a grid, group into series, verify 21 points per series.\n\n6. Convert to data coordinates.\n\nFor classification, note:\n- Open circle: white center, roughly circular extent, radius about 2.5-3.5.\n- Filled circle: dark center, radius about 2.5-3.5.\n- Open star: white center, 5 long arms (max extent 5-6, in between arms about 2).\n- Filled star: dark center, 5 long arms.\n\nFeatures:\n- maxrad: max distance from center to a black pixel within radius 7.\n- For stars: maxrad >= 4.5. For circles: maxrad <= 3.8.\n- Central 3x3 whiteness: > 0.4 → open.\n\nEdge cases: an open circle that a line passes through — the line is black and passes through the center, so the central 3x3 may have some black from the line. But the line is thin (1-2 px) and passes horizontally... Actually, in these plots the line connects the markers and passes through the center. Hmm, the line passes through the center of the marker. So the central 3x3 of an open circle has the line's pixels. Let me check: line thickness about 2px. In a 3x3 window: if the line is horizontal, about 3*2 = 6 black pixels out of 9 → whiteness 3/9=0.33. Hmm, borderline.\n\nFor a filled circle: the line adds on top of the fill.\n\nLet me use a different central test: a ring at radius 1.5-2.5 — for an open circle, the ring is white except where the line passes; for a filled circle, the ring is black everywhere. So: fraction of black on the ring at radius about 2 (e.g., 16 points at radius 2). Open circle: ring mostly white, only about 4-6 points black where the line crosses. Filled circle: all 16 black.\n\nStars: the arms extend beyond radius 3.5; between arms at radius 3.5 is white (for both open and filled stars? no — a filled star has a pentagon core of about radius 2.5, with arms out to about 5.5). Between arms at radius 3.5 of a filled star: white. So the ring test at radius 3.5: filled star has black in about 5 of 16 directions (arms) → 5/16 = 0.31. Open star: the arms are outlined (hollow), so at radius 3.5 the arms are... The outline of a star: the outline is a 10-vertex polygon. At radius 3.5, between an arm's tip and the inner vertex, the outline crosses the ring... For an open star, black pixels at radius about 3.5 exist only where the outline passes through: at each arm's edge and each inner vertex. So 10 crossings × about 1px each ≈ 10 black points out of 16? Hmm, that's a lot.\n\nThis is getting complicated. Let me take a more empirical approach: classify by a combination of (a) maxrad (star vs circle) and (b) area within radius 2.5 (filled vs open). Filled circle area ≈ π*3² ≈ 25-28 px (within radius 3). Open circle area ≈ π*(3²-1.5²) ≈ 21? Hmm, similar. Let me think in terms of \"black fraction within a disk of radius 2\":\n- Filled circle: about 1 (π*4 ≈ 12.6, disk area π*4=12.6)\n- Open circle: disk of radius 2 = ring at r 1-2 → (4-1)=3/π ≈ 0.95? Hmm no: area of ring between r1 and r2 = π(r2²-r1²). Open circle with outer radius 3, inner radius 1.5: within disk of radius 2: π(4-2.25)=5.5 vs disk area 12.6 → 0.44. Filled: 12.6/12.6 = 1.0.\n\nStars: filled star within radius 2: the core pentagon radius about 2.2 → about 1.0. Open star within radius 2: only part of the core outline → about 0.4-0.6.\n\nSo:\n- blackfrac_disk_r2 > 0.75 → filled; < 0.65 → open.\n- maxrad > 4.2 → star; < 3.9 → circle.\n\nLet me measure all candidate markers and tune the thresholds from the data. First detect all candidates and dump the features, then classify.\n\nFor the red and blue series, classification is unnecessary (colors distinguish them), but I do need to detect centers accurately. Red filled circles: use a disk density local maxima. Blue stars: same.\n\nOne more thing to handle: where the red and blue series overlap with each other at low T (the red circle and blue star nearly coincide at 300K). Since the colors differ, the masks stay separate. Good.\n\nAlso axis lines: the plot frame (black border) and tick marks are black — they need to be excluded. The frame is the outer box; the data is inside. Frame lines: detect the border of the plot box: long horizontal/vertical lines. Actually simpler: restrict the data region to x in (100, 330), y in (15, 225), and additionally exclude the frame lines themselves (detect them as rows/columns with very high black density and blank out those 3px bands). The left frame is at x≈... where, and the bottom frame at y≈...? From the calibration, the data area is about x 101-327, y 20-223. The frame is slightly outside that: the left frame at x ≈ 97-99?, the bottom frame at y ≈ 225-227?, the right frame at x ≈ 330-332, the top frame at y ≈ 15-17?\n\nLet me detect: for the black mask, the row with the most black pixels in the y range → bottom frame; the column with the most black in the x range → left frame.\n\nAnd the tick marks extend outside the frame. Inside the frame there should be no ticks. So excluding the frame lines (3px bands) is enough.\n\nOK let me write the script. First, a feature dump of all candidates:\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nfrom PIL import Image\nfrom scipy import ndimage\n\na = np.array(Image.open('images/fig_093.png').convert('RGB')).astype(int)\nr,g,b = a[:,:,0],a[:,:,1],a[:,:,2]\nH,W = a.shape[:2]\nred   = (r>150)&(g<110)&(b<110)\nblue  = (b>150)&(r<110)&(g<110)\nblack = (r<110)&(g<110)&(b<110)\nwhite = (r>170)&(g>170)&(b>170)\n\n# --- find frame lines ---\nrowcnt = black.sum(axis=1)\ncolcnt = black.sum(axis=0)\n# print top candidates\nprint('rows:', [(y,int(rowcnt[y])) for y in range(H) if rowcnt[y]>100])\nprint('cols:', [(x,int(colcnt[x])) for x in range(W) if colcnt[x]>80])\n```\n\nAnd so on. Let me run an exploratory script first."}}
