ing partially overlaps with the top of the filled circle. In that case, the open circle's center is still a local density maximum (the ring's density peaks at the center... no wait! For a ring, the density (black pixels within radius 4) is the same at the center and at the ring position — the convolution is roughly flat across the ring, with a local minimum at the center (the white hole!).\n\nUh oh: for an open circle, the black pixel count in a disk of radius 4 is minimized at the center (the hole) and maximum at the ring. So a disk density local maximum lands on the ring, not the center. Similarly for an open star: density maximum is along the arms/outline, not the center.\n\nHmm. So for open markers, the density maximum lands on the outline. If I take the centroid of the surrounding black pixels, I can get the center: take the local maximum position on the ring, find all black pixels within radius 4 of that maximum, and compute the centroid of that cluster... For a ring, the centroid of black pixels within radius 4 of a point on the ring = biased toward that point. Not great.\n\nBetter method for open markers: white hole detection. The white hole of an open circle is a connected white component of area ~2-8 px, enclosed by black. The white hole of an open star has area ~8-25 px (the pentagonal hole plus the gaps between arms? no — the hole inside a star outline: a pentagon ~ radius 1.5 → area ~4-6 px; plus the notches between arms connect to the outside? In a star outline (pentagram-style 10-vertex polygon), the interior is one connected white region (the pentagon), and the notches between arms are outside the polygon. So hole area ~5-10 px).\n\nBut note: the interior of an open circle can also be partially filled by the black line passing through the center (the line connects the markers and passes through the center). The line splits the white hole into two halves (top and bottom), each ~2-4 px. So the white hole detection should also accept a pair of small holes.\n\nAlternative approach — template matching! I know the exact shapes: filled circle (diameter ~7px), open circle, filled star, open star. I can crop the legend markers as templates! The legend markers are clean, isolated, and drawn in the same style. Then do normalized cross-correlation against the data region.\n\nThis is elegant:\n- Legend templates: red circle at (93,30), blue star at (93,48), open circle at (93,64.5), filled circle at (93,82), open star at (93,98), filled star at (93,115.5).\n- But wait — legend markers are connected by lines (each legend entry has a line through the marker). Looking at the crop: yes, each legend marker has a short horizontal line segment going through it. So the template would include line stubs. Still, it should work for matching — in the data too, the line passes through the marker (though at various angles).\n- Template matching with the marker's center: the marker part dominates the correlation.\n\nHmm, but the line angle differs between the legend (horizontal) and the data (diagonal). The correlation score might be lower, but since I'm comparing the same marker type, relative ranking should still be correct.\n\nActually, a simpler and more robust method: let me combine approaches:\n\n1. **Red circles**: density peaks of the red mask (filled circles — the density at the center is maximal since it's filled). Clean.\n2. **Blue stars**: blue filled stars. Filled star: center is black (core). A disk of radius 4 at the center includes the core + 5 arm stubs → maximal at the center. Density peaks should work.\n3. **Black filled circles and filled stars**: erode the black mask with a 3x3 (the line disappears), then find connected components = filled marker cores. Centroid = center. Then classify star vs circle by the maxrad of the original black pixels around the core.\n4. **Black open circles and open stars**: find small enclosed white holes (white connected components of area 2-40 px, not touching the frame/background). For each hole cluster, center = centroid of the black ring surrounding it. Then classify star vs circle by the shape of the surrounding black (directional extent).\n\nOverlaps (open circle near filled circle, etc.): the white hole of the open circle might be partially covered by the adjacent filled circle. The hole detection might shrink or split. The center estimate from the centroid of the surrounding ring is biased... let me think. At 300K: open circle center ~ (101, 201), filled circle ~ (101, 204-205). Filled circle radius ~3.5 → top of filled circle at y≈201.5 — overlaps with the bottom of the open circle ring (bottom of open circle at y≈204). So the bottom arc of the open circle is eaten into/merged with the top of the filled circle. The white hole of the open circle is the top part (y≈198-201, x≈99-103) — the hole is still partially enclosed by the black of the ring's top + the top of the filled circle. It might still be detectable as a small white component.\n\nThis is getting quite complex. Given the small image and just 4 black series × 21 points, a hybrid approach using manual verification would work too:\n\nFor each of the 21 x positions (300, 320, ..., 700 K → pixels 100.8, 112.1, 123.4, ..., 326.85), for each of the 4 black series, find the y by shape-specific local search. Since I can see the curves in the enlarged image, I can also visually verify each column.\n\nActually — let me step back and use a cleaner global approach: **per-series curve tracing**. The black curves are smooth and ordered. For each x column in the data region, I can find the vertical profile of black and identify the bands. Between series, the bands separate. The 4 black series + 2 colored series = 6 curves. If I detect band centers per column, I can trace the curves...\n\nBut the bands merge where markers overlap (e.g., at low T where open + filled circles are 4px apart → one band).\n\nOK, let me try the erosion + white hole approach first and see what I get; I'll dump all features