e outside through a gap in the ring (e.g., a white pixel connecting to background at y121 x284-285 → then it's not an enclosed hole).\n\nLet me check the white pixels in (282-287, 118-122): y120: 284 is white (missing from black list); y121: 284,285 are white. Is (284,121) connected to the outside? Neighbors of (284,121): (284,120) white, (283,121) black, (285,121) white, (284,122) black... (285,121): neighbors (285,120) black, (286,121) black, (285,122) black, (284,121) white. So the white cluster = {(284,120), (285,121), (284,121)}? (284,121): neighbors (283,121) black, (285,121) white, (284,120) white, (284,122) black. So the cluster is 3px: (284,120), (284,121), (285,121). All 4-neighbors are black → enclosed! Area 3 — but my filter required area>=2... area 3 should have passed! But the region filter: the f93_cc.py region was x0-2 < x < x1 → 276 < 284.5 < 332 ✓, 85 < 120.5 < 135 ✓, 2<=3<=40 ✓. So it should have been printed! Unless the cluster is larger (connected to more white).\n\nHmm wait, in the original f93_final.py's hole detection (region x 76-350, y 18-228, area 2-60), the holes printed near x 284-299 were: (284.5,99.5) area 12, (298.8,97.0) area 10. No (284,120). So the 3px cluster at (284-285, 120-121) must be connected to the outside → not enclosed → part of the background.\n\nOK, so Marker C = a ring-like structure with the center white but not fully enclosed — i.e., an open circle with a gap in the ring (or a white center leaking out). This is an open circle (EuZn2Sb2) at 625K at (284.5, 120.3)??\n\nBut wait — then there would be two open circles at 625K: (284.5, 99.5) and (284.5, 120.3)?! That can't be.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider. What if (284.5, 99.5) is actually the open star (YbCd2Sb2), and (284.5, 120.3) is the open circle (EuZn2Sb2)?!\n\nLet me look at Marker A's shape again:\n- y97: 283,284,285,286 (top, 4px wide)\n- y98: 282,283, 286,287\n- y99: 282, 287,288,289,290 (right side continues to 290!)\n- y100: 282, 287\n- y101: 281,282, 286,287\n- y102: 279,280, 283,284,285,286\n- y103: 278\n\nIf it's an open circle at (284.5, 99.5): the left arc at x279-282 ✓, right arc at x287-289 ✓, top at y97 ✓, bottom at y101-102 ✓. Fairly circular: width x278-290 (12px), height y97-103 (7px) — hmm, a bit flat, but the line stubs add width. The hole is 12px area, at (284.5, 99.5) ✓ enclosed. This reads as an open circle.\n\nAnd Marker C at (284.5, 120.3): if it's a filled circle (EuCd2Sb2), it should be a solid ~9px blob. But I see holes at (284,120), (285,121), (284,121) with a leak. And erosion found no core. So Marker C is a hollow-ish ring = open circle??\n\nHmm, wait, wait. Let me double-check: is Marker C perhaps a filled circle whose interior has a white pixel due to JPEG artifacts? The image is PNG but was probably exported from a PDF — should be clean. A 3px white leak inside a 9px black blob is odd for a filled marker.\n\nAlternatively, Marker C could be an open star at (284.5, 120.3)! Let me look: star centered at (284.5, 120.3): top tip pointing up at (284-285, 117-118) ✓ (I see 284,285 at y118 and 284 at y117); left arm at (280-282, 121-124) ✓; right arm at (287-289, 117-119) ✓ (288,289 at y117; 287,288 at y118); lower-left arm at (281-282, 124-126)?? (278,279 at y125-126 = line); lower-right arm at (287, 124)? Hmm: y124: 287. And y122-123: 282-286 (center region, partially filled).\n\nAn open star outline: a 10-vertex polygon — the center should be white, but the outline passes near the center. At this resolution (star radius ~5), the center white region of an open star is a pentagon of radius ~1.5-2 → area ~4-7px. Here the center white = 3px (284,120), (284,121), (285,121) — a bit small but plausible.\n\nBut why does the center white leak out?? Let me check the neighbors of (285,121) again: (286,121)? Black list for y121: 282,283,286 → 286,121 is black. (285,120): black (y120: 285 is in the list). (285,122): black (y122: 282-286). (284,120): white; (284,121): white; (283,121): black; (284,122): black. So the white cluster {(284,120), (284,121), (285,121)} is enclosed by black on all sides. It cannot leak out!\n\nSo why wasn't it in the hole list?? Let me recheck f93_final.py's hole region filter: `if not (76<cx<350 and 18<cy<228): continue` → (284.3, 120.7) passes. `if cx<206 and cy<136: continue` → cx=284, not <206 → passes. `area<2 or area>60` → area 3 passes. So it should have been printed... unless the white label component containing (284,120) is connected to the background through another path I haven't checked — e.g., a diagonal connection? No, ndimage.label uses 4-connectivity by default... actually, scipy's default structure is a cross (4-connectivity). Hmm, but wait — if the white cluster connects to the outside through a path I missed, that's different. The 8-neighbors of (284,120): (283,119) black, (285,119) black, (283,120) black, (285,120) black, (283,121) black, (285,121) white, (284,119) black, (284,121) white. 8-neighbors of (285,121): (284,120) white, (286,120) black, (284,122) black, (286,122) black, (285,120) black, (285,122) black, (284,121) white, (286,121) black. 8-neighbors of (284,121): covered. So even with 8-connectivity, it's enclosed. So the area-3 white hole at (284.3, 120.7) exists. Then why wasn't it printed??\n\nOh, wait. The black mask in f93_final.py was modified: `m[:136,:206]=False` etc. — no wait, that's the black mask, not white. The white mask in f93_final.py wasn't masked (white = raw value). The hole at (284, 120) — raw white... the white mask includes all white pixels; the background is one huge component; the hole is a separate small component. It should be printed!\n\nHmm, maybe... the white cluster is actually larger — maybe (284,120) connects to (284,119)? (284,119): y119 list: 283,284,285,286,287 → black. No.\n\nLet me verify directly in code — check the label of (284,120) and its area. Also output the actual pixel colors there to make sure my black/white thresholds aren't misclassifying (e.g., (284,120) might be a mid-gray pixel (150,150,150) — neither black nor white by my thresholds! Then \"enclosed white\" is an illusion: the center might be gray (anti-aliasing) and connect to the outside through more gray pixels!).\n\nThat's probably it: the interior of Marker C is a mid-gray pixel (from anti-aliased blending), which I was neither counting as black nor as white. So the \"hole\" is actually a gray region, and the \"black ring\" is partially gray. The actual pixel values matter. Let me dump the raw RGB of this small region."}}
