{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_192","type":"reasoning","text":"Both stars look almost identical—both are filled 5-pointed stars. There's no clear visual distinction between YbCd₂Sb₂ (star A) and YbZn₂Sb₂ (star B). In other words, the two star curves need to be distinguished not by the shape of the markers, but by their position (which curve is which).\n\nThis is the really hard part. Let me think about the physics. This is a figure of thermal conductivity × temperature (cT) vs. temperature for skutterudite compounds:\n- EuZn₁.₈Cd₀.₂Sb₂ (red filled circles)—highest\n- YbCd₁.₆Zn₀.₄Sb₂ (blue stars)\n- EuZn₂Sb₂ (black open circles)\n- EuCd₂Sb₂ (black filled circles)\n- YbCd₂Sb₂ (black stars)\n- YbZn₂Sb₂ (black stars)\n\nThe y-axis is cT (probably lattice thermal conductivity, or total thermal conductivity times temperature).\n\nNow, at high temperatures the curves separate. From the 8× zoomed image, at the right edge (high T), from top to bottom:\n- Blue stars (top)\n- Red circles\n- Black open circles\n- Black filled circles\n- One black star curve\n- The lowest black star curve\n\nWait, let me recount. Looking at the 8× image at high T (right side), the vertical order from top to bottom is:\n1. Blue stars (top)\n2. Red circles\n3. Black open circles (hollow)\n4. Black filled circles\n5. Black star (upper star)\n6. Black star (lower star, bottom)\n\nHmm, but I need to identify which black star is YbCd₂Sb₂ and which is YbZn₂Sb₂.\n\nActually, both star series use the same star marker shape. So they have to be distinguished by their vertical position relative to the other black series.\n\nPhysical reasoning: This looks like a study of thermal conductivity in rare-earth-filled skutterudites. Generally, Yb-filled skutterudites have lower thermal conductivity than Eu-filled ones. And Cd substitution affects this too.\n\nBut which is lower, YbCd₂Sb₂ or YbZn₂Sb₂? Zn and Cd have different phonon effects. Actually, in skutterudites, the choice of the filling atom (Yb vs. Eu) has a bigger effect than the choice of the metalloid-site atom (Zn vs. Cd).\n\nSince there's no ground truth, I can't be 100% sure which star curve is which. But the benchmark compares against hidden ground truth. I need to make my best guess.\n\nLet me think about the order in the legend. Legend order (top to bottom):\n1. EuZn₁.₈Cd₀.₂Sb₂\n2. YbCd₁.₆Zn₀.₄Sb₂\n3. EuZn₂Sb₂\n4. EuCd₂Sb₂\n5. YbCd₂Sb₂\n6. YbZn₂Sb₂\n\nLegend order often reflects the data order in the source, and frequently (though not always) matches the curve order at high temperature (top to bottom). If the legend order matches the top-to-bottom order at high T, then:\n- Top = EuZn₁.₈Cd₀.₂Sb₂ (red)\n- 2nd = YbCd₁.₆Zn₀.₄Sb₂ (blue)\n- 3rd = EuZn₂Sb₂ (open circles)\n- 4th = EuCd₂Sb₂ (filled circles)\n- 5th = YbCd₂Sb₂ (star)\n- 6th = YbZn₂Sb₂ (star, lowest)\n\nBut wait—in the 8× image, at high T the red circles appear to be below the blue stars. Let me double-check. Looking at the zoomed image, in the top-right corner, the blue stars reach the very top (highest). The red circles are just below. So at high T, blue > red. But in the legend, red (EuZn₁.₈Cd₀.₂Sb₂) is listed first. So the legend order doesn't match the top-to-bottom order at high T (if blue is on top).\n\nHmm, this means I can't rely on legend order = curve order. Let me look more carefully.\n\nActually, at low T (left side), red and blue are close together, with red perhaps slightly on top or overlapping. At high T, blue overtakes red and becomes the highest.\n\nLet me reexamine. This is really a hard figure. Given the ambiguity, let me proceed with the following policy:\n- Red = EuZn₁.₈Cd₀.₂Sb₂\n- Blue = YbCd₁.₆Zn₀.₄Sb₂\n- Black open circles = EuZn₂Sb₂\n- Black filled circles = EuCd₂Sb₂\n- Black star (upper) = ?\n- Black star (lower) = ?\n\nAs for the two stars, I'll use physical knowledge. Let me think about which compound has lower cT.\n\nActually, this figure is from a published paper. Let me think—this looks like it's from a pape