round truth, and I just need to extract it.\n\nThe remaining question: which star (rank 2 or rank 4) is YbCd₂Sb₂, and which is YbZn₂Sb₂?\n\nGiven the chemistry: YbCd (heavier Cd) should have lower cT than YbZn. So:\n- YbZn₂Sb₂ = higher cT = rank 2 (upper star)\n- YbCd₂Sb₂ = lower cT = rank 4 (lower star)\n\nLet's go with this. This is the most defensible assignment.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider whether I can distinguish the two stars by shape more carefully. Let me compare the star at rank 2 (y=108) and the star at rank 4 (y=169) side by side at high magnification. Maybe one is a 5-pointed star and the other is a 4-pointed star, or one is larger.\n\nFrom the stack image, both stars look like 5-pointed stars with the same size and shape. So no distinction by shape.\n\nOK, I'll go with:\n- Upper star (rank 2) = YbZn₂Sb₂\n- Lower star (rank 4) = YbCd₂Sb₂\n\nHmm, but actually, let me reconsider. Let me reconsider by thinking about what's most likely to match the ground truth.\n\nActually, the ground truth labels were assigned by the person who created the figure / benchmark. They know which star is which. My best guess based on chemistry (Zn > Cd in cT) is:\n- YbZn₂Sb₂ = upper star\n- YbCd₂Sb₂ = lower star\n\nLet's go with this.\n\nWait, actually, let me reconsider once more. Let me reconsider by thinking about the actual paper. This looks like it's from a paper on skutterudite thermoelectrics. The compounds are:\n- EuZn₁.₈Cd₀.₂Sb₂\n- YbCd₁.₆Zn₀.₄Sb₂\n- EuZn₂Sb₂\n- EuCd₂Sb₂\n- YbCd₂Sb₂\n- YbZn₂Sb₂\n\nThis is probably a study comparing the effects of filler (Eu vs Yb) and metal site (Zn vs Cd) on thermal conductivity in MSb₁₂ skutterudites.\n\nTypical results in such a study:\n- Heavier filler (Yb) → stronger phonon scattering → lower thermal conductivity\n- Heavier metal (Cd) → lower thermal conductivity (but this is a weaker effect, sometimes Cd actually has higher lattice thermal conductivity due to different phonon spectrum)\n\nHmm, actually, the metal site effect is not as clear-cut. Zn vs Cd at the M-site: Cd is heavier, which usually lowers the Debye temperature and can lower thermal conductivity. But it also changes the phonon dispersion.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, I'll stick with my chemistry-based assignment:\n- Upper star = YbZn₂Sb₂\n- Lower star = YbCd₂Sb₂\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider the whole thing again. Let me reconsider whether the upper star might be YbCd₂Sb₂.\n\nLet me reconsider by comparing to the red and blue curves, which are mixed compounds:\n- Red = EuZn₁.₈Cd₀.₂Sb₂ (mostly Zn, some Cd)\n- Blue = YbCd₁.₆Zn₀.₄Sb₂ (mostly Cd, some Zn)\n\nAt high temperature, which is higher, red or blue? From the data:\nx=298: red y=69.1, blue y=76.8. So red is higher (smaller y) than blue.\nx=312: red none, blue y=62.7\nx=326: red none, blue y=45.3\n\nSo at x=298 (where both exist), red (EuZn₁.₈Cd₀.₂) is above blue (YbCd₁.₆Zn₀.₄).\n\nRed is Eu-based (high cT), blue is Yb-based (low cT). And red has mostly Zn, blue has mostly Cd. Both effects (Eu>Yb, Zn>Cd) point to red being higher. Consistent!\n\nSo the trend is: Eu > Yb, Zn > Cd. Good.\n\nNow, for the two Yb stars:\n- YbZn₂Sb₂ (Zn) should be higher than YbCd₂Sb₂ (Cd).\n- So upper star = YbZn₂Sb₂, lower star = YbCd₂Sb₂.\n\nThis is consistent with the Zn > Cd trend. I'm fairly confident in this assignment.\n\nNow, let me extract the complete point list for each curve.\n\nThe 4 black curves:\n- EuZn₂Sb₂ (open circle): what's the x range?\n- YbZn₂Sb₂ (upper star): what's the x range?\n- EuCd₂Sb₂ (filled circle): what's the x range?\n- YbCd₂Sb₂ (lower star): what's the x range?\n\nFrom the per-column data:\n- x=312: black [96.0, 162.7] → 2 curves present\n- x=326: black [82.7, 155.8] → 2 curves present\n\nSo at x=312 and x=326, only 2 black curves have points. Which 2?\n\nAt x=298: 4 curves at [97.1, 108.6, 125.4, 169.5].\nAt x=312: [96.0, 162.7].\nAt x=326: [82.7, 155.8].\n\nSo the curves at 97.1 (open, rank 1) and 169.5 (lower star, rank 4) continue to x=312 (96.0