e actual plot order (at high T) is: open (EuZn), star, filled (EuCd), star. So a star is between open and filled. This means the legend order doesn't match the plot order (the curves cross).\n\nSo I can't rely on the legend order. I need to determine which star is which by the physics or by marker shape.\n\nLet me look at the marker shapes of the two stars. In the montage, both stars look like the same shape (5-pointed filled stars). Let me compare more carefully.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider. Let me look at the two star markers in the plot (rank 2 and rank 4) and compare them to the two star markers in the legend (YbCd₂Sb₂ and YbZn₂Sb₂).\n\nHmm, but both stars in the legend look the same. So the two star curves are probably the same marker type (both 5-pointed filled stars), and the only difference is position.\n\nSo I have to assign YbCd₂Sb₂ and YbZn₂Sb₂ to the top star and bottom star.\n\nPhysically: which has higher cT, YbCd₂Sb₂ or YbZn₂Sb₂?\n\nLet me think. In skutterudites, the thermal conductivity depends on the filler and the metal site. Heavier atoms → lower phonon frequency → but also more scattering. Generally, substituting Zn with Cd (heavier) tends to lower the lattice thermal conductivity (more phonon scattering from mass contrast). So YbCd₂Sb₂ (with Cd) should have lower cT than YbZn₂Sb₂ (with Zn).\n\nSo:\n- YbZn₂Sb₂ = higher cT = top star (rank 2)\n- YbCd₂Sb₂ = lower cT = bottom star (rank 4)\n\nWait, but I should double-check with the Eu pair. EuZn₂Sb₂ (open, rank 1, top) vs EuCd₂Sb₂ (filled, rank 3, below the top star). So EuZn (Zn) is on top of EuCd (Cd). This is consistent with Zn > Cd in cT!\n\nSo the pattern is: Zn compounds have higher cT than Cd compounds.\n- EuZn₂Sb₂ (Zn) > EuCd₂Sb₂ (Cd) ✓\n- YbZn₂Sb₂ (Zn) > YbCd₂Sb₂ (Cd)\n\nSo the top star = YbZn₂Sb₂, the bottom star = YbCd₂Sb₂.\n\nNow, the overall order (high T, top to bottom):\n1. EuZn₁.₈Cd₀.₂Sb₂ (red) - wait, where is red?\n\nLet me reconsider the full ordering including red and 