arkers into 4 curves and assign each curve a marker type.\n\nLet me do a systematic pixel analysis. Let me first detect the plot area and grid.\n\nLet me write a comprehensive analysis script.\n\nThe prior model's confident extractions:\n- RED (15) px_y: 191.4,185.2,179.5,170.5,162.8,153.6,144.6,134.8,125.3,115.7,104.9,94.7,84.7,77.7,69.0 at x=101..298 (15 cols)\n- BLUE (17) px_y: 194.6,189.6,184.7,178.4,171.7,164.7,157.3,150.0,140.5,131.5,121.5,111.6,100.8,89.7,76.9,62.6,45.2 at x=101..326 (17 cols)\n- OPEN / EuZn₂Sb₂ (17) px_y: 196.9,191.3,187.1,180.0,168.8,160.6,153.0,144.7,135.9,126.8,123.4,115.0,106.7,99.8,97.1,96.0,82.7 at x=101..326\n- LOWER STAR / YbCd₂Sb₂ (17) px_y: 210.7,216.3,215.1,213.0,211.4,209.2,206.5,203.6,200.5,196.3,192.5,187.0,181.8,175.6,169.5,162.7,155.8 at x=101..326\n\nThe two middle black curves (upper star = YbZn, filled = EuCd) are ambiguous at x=101..298.\n\nWait, but the prior model said:\n- upper star (YbZn₂Sb₂): x=101..298 → 15 pts\n- filled (EuCd₂Sb₂): x=101..298 → 15 pts\n\nHmm, but I should double check these assignments. Let me verify the chemistry ordering. \n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider the legend. Looking at the zoomed legend:\n- Row 1: red circle — EuZn₁.₈Cd₀.₂Sb₂\n- Row 2: blue star — YbCd₁.₆Zn₀.₄Sb₂\n- Row 3: open circle — EuZn₂Sb₂\n- Row 4: filled circle — EuCd₂Sb₂\n- Row 5: star (open star) — YbCd₂Sb₂\n- Row 6: star (filled star) — YbZn₂Sb₂\n\nNow, which physical curve is which? Let me think about the physics of these half-Heusler / CaAlSi-type antiferromagnets (EuZn₂Sb₂ etc. are double half-Heuslers). The Curie/Néel temperature cT depends on composition.\n\nEu-based compounds: EuZn₁.₈Cd₀.₂Sb₂ (highest cT), EuZn₂Sb₂ (3rd), EuCd₂Sb₂.\nYb-based: YbCd₁.₆Zn₀.₄Sb₂ (2nd), YbCd₂Sb₂, YbZn₂Sb₂.\n\nGenerally, replacing Eu with Yb lowers cT (Yb is lighter, different magnetic coupling). And replacing Zn with Cd also tends to lower cT (heavier, more disorder).\n\nSo ranking by cT (high to low), the observed order from the plot:\n1