ax radius of black pixels vs the min radius, or the variance of radius. For a star, radius varies a lot (points stick out). For a circle, radius is roughly constant.\n\nAlso, a star has a \"pointy\" top. Let me use the ratio of area to convex hull, or the number of black pixels at the outer edge.\n\nLet me measure:\n- fill: fraction of black in central 3x3 (or 5x5). Open markers → low center fill. Filled → high.\n- shape: For circle, the marker is round. For star, there are 5 points. \n\nSimplest robust: look at the \"extent\" - a star has black pixels reaching further from center (the points), especially at certain angles. A circle's max extent is more uniform.\n\nLet me compute: the max distance of any black pixel from center, and the angular distribution. For a star, the max distance is large at 5 specific angles (the points) and smaller in between. For a circle, it's roughly constant.\n\nMetric: variance of (max radius per angle bin). Star → high variance. Circle → low variance.\n\nLet me code this. Let me fix the syntax.\n\nActually, let me use a cleaner approach for circle vs star: \n- Compute the black mask within radius R around center.\n- A filled circle: roughly a disk. \n- A star: has points. The star's silhouette has a smaller \"inscribed circle\". \n\nGood metric: the ratio of (area of black) to (area of bounding disk of radius rmax). A filled circle fills ~78% of its disk. A star fills much less (~30-40%) because of the concave notches.\n\nBut that's for filled markers. For open markers, it's a ring/outline.\n\nLet me handle it per case. Let me just measure and look at the numbers for the 4 legend markers, then set thresholds.\n\nLet me fix the syntax and run the classifier on the 4 legend markers to get distinguishing features."}}
