olid blob, rounded → filled circle. Yes, EuCd₂Sb₂.\n\nThe 108.1 one:\n```\n..........#\n.........##\n....##..##.\n....##.##..\n##########.\n..##..##...\n...####....\n..#####....\n.###..##...\n###....#...\n#..........\n```\nThe \"##########\" wide band in the middle, with spread-out parts top and bottom → this looks like a STAR (filled star, YbZn₂Sb₂). The wide middle band with the points spreading = star.\n\nActually, both look somewhat like they could be circles or stars. Let me look more carefully at a cleaner position. Let me find where these two middle curves are well-separated and render them.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Let me look at the vertical ordering more carefully and use the marker shape to identify each curve, then track each curve across T.\n\nLet me render the 4 black markers at T=600 (where there are 4 distinct: 107.0, 131.5, 140.5, 182.3) — wait that's the clean 4.\n\nT=600 (x=270): 107.0, 131.5, 140.5, 182.3\n- 107.0 = open circle (EuZn₂Sb₂) topmost\n- 131.5 = ?\n- 140.5 = ?\n- 182.3 = star (bottom)\n\nLet me render these 4.\n\nActually, let me take yet another approach that's cleaner. Let me identify each of the 4 black curves by:\n1. Their marker type (open circle / filled circle / open star / filled star)\n2. Track them across T by continuity\n\nThe key is to correctly identify the marker type of each curve. Let me render clean markers where each curve is isolated.\n\nLet me look at the leftmost (T=300) where the curves are most bunched, and the rightmost (T=650, 675, 700) where they're most spread.\n\nAt T=700, I only have 2 curves (open circle + one star). Let me identify that star.\n\nLet me render the T=700 bottom star (cy=155.8) and compare to open_star vs filled_star legend."}}
