.6 = 15 points.\n\nx temps: \n101 → 300.4\n115.2 → 325.2\n129.0 → 349.8\n143.7 → 375.8\n157.6 → 400.7\n171.5 → 425.6\n185.5 → 450.5\n199.7 → 475.7\n213.8 → 500.9\n228.1 → 526.2\n241.7 → 549.8\n256.1 → 575.4\n270.5 → 601.0\n284.5 → 625.0\n298.6 → 650.1\n\nSo red data is at 300, 325, 350, 375, 400, ..., 650. That's 15 points (300 to 650 in steps of 25). Interesting — red ends at 650, not 700.\n\nBlue: 300, 325, 350, 375, 400, ..., 700 = 17 points (ends at 700).\n\nSo the series have different x ranges! Red goes 300-650, blue goes 300-700. Let me verify blue's first points. Blue detected: 101(300), 115(325), 137(merged 350+375), 157.5(400), ..., 326(700). So blue: 300,325,350,375,400,425,450,475,500,525,550,575,600,625,650,675,700 = 17 points.\n\nHmm, so the data points are at 25K intervals starting from 300K. That's a clean pattern. Red has 15 (up to 650), blue has 17 (up to 700).\n\nWait, but let me double check red's count. Let me re-examine. Actually, maybe red also has a point at 675 and 700 that I'm missing, or maybe it genuinely stops at 650. Let me look at the image — the red curve is the top one and it ends at the top-right. In the rendered image, red goes up to about 1.2-1.3 zT at the right end. The last red point (298.6, 68.8) → x=650, y=(222.7-68.8)/202.75*1.4 = 153.9/202.75*1.4 = 1.063. And blue last (326.4, 45.1) → x=700, y=(222.7-45.1)/202.75*1.4 = 177.6/202.75*1.4 = 1.227.\n\nOK so I need to carefully determine the exact number and positions of points for each series. This is going to require careful detection.\n\nLet me reconsider the whole thing. The 4 black series are the hard part:\n- EuZn₂Sb₂ (black open circles)\n- EuCd₂Sb₂ (black filled circles)\n- YbCd₂Sb₂ (black open stars)\n- YbZn₂Sb₂ (black filled stars)\n\nThese are all black, in the lower part of the plot. Distinguishing open vs filled circles and open vs filled stars, and separating the 4 curves.\n\nThis is genuinely difficult. Let me look at the image more carefully in the lower region to