ch: use Python to detect the marker positions:\n\n1. Red circles: red pixels, cluster → centers.\n2. Blue stars: blue pixels, cluster → centers.\n3. Black shapes: circles (open/filled) and stars (open/filled).\n   - Open circle: a ring — a black circle outline with a white center.\n   - Filled circle: a black disk.\n   - Open star: a black star outline with a white center.\n   - Filled star: a black star shape (solid).\n\nDistinguishing stars from circles: stars have a characteristic shape — a 5-pointed star has corners/points; a circle is round. For a template-free approach: measure the distribution of black pixels within the connected component (or within a local window around the marker). Or detect the white holes: both open circles and open stars have white interiors; filled markers don't.\n\nActually, a simpler approach: for each marker position, examine the black pixels in a window. For a star, the black pixels spread out more (larger bounding box) relative to the white hole. Hmm.\n\nBetter method: for each black connected component (markers are separated by thin lines), for a marker on a line, the component contains the line. Alternative: for each column x, find clusters of black pixels... hmm.\n\nLet me think about a robust algorithm:\n\n1. Find red pixels (R>150, G<100, B<100). Cluster with DBSCAN-like or connected components → red marker centers. Red circles are filled disks; each marker is a cluster. Lines are also red but thin (~1-2 px), while markers are ~8-10 px wide. With connected components, markers+lines all become one component. So better: count red pixels per column (or per small window) and find local peaks where the count is high (marker diameter >> line width). Or use morphological erosion: erode the mask with a 3x3 kernel a few times; the line disappears and the disks remain.\n\n2. Blue similarly.\n\n3. For black markers: erode the black mask; filled circles, filled stars remain (as smaller blobs); open circles/stars (outlines) shrink to ring