specific angles; between the points the radius is smaller. For a star, the variance of the radius at 5 angles is high; for a circle it's low.\n\nAlso note: markers lie on lines (connecting lines), so there are extra black pixels along the line passing through the marker. For open circles on lines: the line enters from both sides, and at the left and right edges there will be black pixels touching the circle. This is fine for classification.\n\nBut one big issue: overlapping markers. At low T (300 K), the 6 series are close together: e.g., red 0.19, blue 0.18, open circle 0.17, filled circle 0.11, open star 0.09, filled star 0.02. Some markers may overlap (blue star and red circle at 300 K are very close: 0.18 vs 0.19). In the enlarged image at 300 K: I can see a red circle at ~0.19 with a blue star overlapping just below/around it? They look almost overlapping.\n\nAlso at intermediate temperatures the open circle and blue star curves cross around ~550-600 K. And filled circle and open star curves are close and cross around ~500-550 K? Let me see: at 550 K: open circle ~0.63?, filled circle ~0.49?, open star ~0.50?, ... hmm.\n\nOK, my plan is this:\n\nStep 1: Detect red marker centers (via erosion/density).\nStep 2: Detect blue marker centers.\nStep 3: Detect black markers: density peaks of the black mask in the plot area (excluding the legend area).\nStep 4: Classify each black marker as {open circle, filled circle, open star, filled star}.\nStep 5: Group by x-position (all series probably share the same T values: 300, 325, ..., 700 → 17 points). For each x, there should be up to 4 black markers (one per black series) + red + blue. Then assign to series by y-order continuity and shape.\n\nWait, but the x-values may not be the same for each series: the red series ends at 650, blue ends at 700, open circle ends at 650?, filled circle ends at 650?, open star ends at 700, filled star ends at 700. Hmm, let me look at the image again to confirm.\n\nFrom the enlarged image