n't exactly match the actual star center — the true star center might be at y=99, or the star might be drawn at a slightly different position. Well, whatever.\n\nKey point: open star and filled star are both mostly filled stars with small white gaps somewhere; distinguishing them requires detecting the white gaps:\n- Filled star's bottom: the legs are separated by a wider white gap (`#....#`), and there's a `##.##` row.\n- Open star: a 1px white gap at the center row `#######.#######`.\n\nBoth have white gaps! Hmm.\n\nLet me think about how these are drawn. In matplotlib, the '*' marker with `fillstyle='none'` gives an open star (outline only), and filled gives a solid star. The outline should be a thin stroke around the star's polygon. But here both look almost solid...\n\nNo, wait. Maybe the \"open\" star is an outline star (stroke only), and the star is small, so the stroke fills in most of the star, leaving small white areas: the open star has white notches at the concave vertices (the inner corners of the star), plus the center may remain white. The filled star is solid.\n\nLooking at open star's art again:\n```\nrow91: .......#.......\nrow92: .......#.......\nrow93: .......##......\nrow94: ...#########...\nrow95: #######.#######\nrow96: .....#####.....\nrow97: .....#####.....\nrow98: .....##..#.....\nrow99: ..........#....\n```\nWhite pixels inside the star's extent: row 95 column 90 (`.`, center), and row 98 columns 91,92 (`..`), row 99... this white pattern looks like the interior of the star's outline is partially white.\n\nFilled star's art:\n```\nrow112: .......#.......\nrow113: .......#.......\nrow114: .......##......\nrow115: ...#########...\nrow116: ###############\nrow117: ###############\nrow118: .....#####.....\nrow119: .....##.##.....\nrow120: .....#....#....\n```\nWhite inside: row 119 column 90 (`.`, 1px), row 120 columns 90-93 (`....`, 4px).\n\nSo both stars are mostly filled. Differences:\n1. Filled star is taller/wider (rows 112-120 = 9 rows +