tually, is (91,98) really the center? Let me reconsider. The star spans rows 91-99, so the center is about 95. Wait, rows 91 to 99 = 9 rows, center = 95. But the test point is (91,98)?\n\nLet me recompute. From zz_classify.py, the test point for the hollow star is (91,98). Let me look at the actual vertical extent. From the art (cy=98, h=7, so rows 91-105):\n```\nrow91: .......#.......   (col 90 = top vertex)\nrow92: .......#.......\nrow93: .......##......\nrow94: ...#########...\nrow95: #######.#######\nrow96: .....#####.....\nrow97: .....#####.....\nrow98: .....##..#.....\nrow99: ..........#....\nrow100+: all dots\n```\nSo the star spans rows 91-99 (9 rows). Center = (91+99)/2 = 95. So the true center is y≈95, not 98. The test point (91,98) is 3 px below the true center. That's why \"center=0.75\" was measured at the wrong location.\n\nLet me redo this with the correct center y=95.\nHollow star, center (91,95):\n- Center 2x2 (rows 94-95, cols 90-91):\n  row94 cols 90,91: `...#########...` col90=#, col91=#. → #\n  row95 cols 90,91: `#######.#######` col90=., col91=#. → .#\n  So center = 3/4 = 0.75.\n\nHmm. Let me carefully redo the feature analysis with the correct center. First, let me programmatically find the exact bounding box of each legend marker, compute the true center, and extract clean features.\n\nLet me write a cleaner script."}}
