{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_469","type":"reasoning","text":"Legend markers (excluding the line stubs that extend to x=80 and 101):\n- Red circle: rows 25-32, center y ≈ 28.5\n- Blue star: rows 42-49, center y ≈ 45.5\n- Open circle: rows 61-66, center y ≈ 63.5\n- Filled circle: rows 78-84, center y ≈ 81\n- Open star: rows 94-101, center y ≈ 97.5\n- Filled star: rows 111-119, center y ≈ 115\n\nSo the earlier test points (63, 81, 98, 115) were roughly correct.\n\nNext, the plan for the entire extraction. Let's design a robust pipeline:\n\n**Step 1: Plot area and legend exclusion.**\nFrom the frame: let's find the plot's frame. The frame lines were around x 76-77 (left axis) and x 353-354 (right)? Rows y≈20 (top) and y≈227 (bottom)? From the earlier cluster scan at x=327: clusters at row 20 (top frame) and rows 226-229 (bottom frame + tick marks?). Let's identify the frame precisely: scan the long black horizontal/vertical lines.\n\n**Step 2: Red circles.**\nRed mask; the red line is 1-2px thick, the markers are about 8px disks. Use column-wise density: for each column, count red pixels in the plot rows. Marker regions have count >= 4 or so. Then group consecutive columns into marker blobs, and for each blob take the x range, and for the center x, find the centroid y. Actually simpler: erode the red mask once (a 1-2px line disappears under 1 erosion? A 1px-wide line disappears completely under one erosion with a 3x3 structuring element; a 2px-wide line also disappears under 1 erosion, because the erosion requires all 8 neighbors... a 2px horizontal line: a pixel in the line needs all 8 neighbors to be black — the pixel above is not black → disappears. Good). Then label the components: each remaining blob = a circle (about a 4-6px disk). Centroid = center.\n\nBut wait — the red line between markers: if the line is 2px thick and markers are dense, erosion leaves only the disks.\n\n**Step 3: Blue stars.**\nSame approach: erode the blue mask. The blue star is almost filled (like the black filled star, most of it black) → erosion leaves a star-shaped blob (small). Centroid. But the centroid of a star is offset from the center. From the legend: blue star bbox rows 42-49 (center 45.5); the centroid after erosion will be slightly below the center. We can correct: compute the offset using the legend marker itself! The legend blue star (without line) is a clean template: compute the erosion blob centroid, and the offset from the bbox center → apply the same offset to the plot markers. Similarly for the black filled star.\n\n**Step 4: Black markers.**\nThis is the tricky one. 4 types: open circle (ring), filled circle (disk), open star (star with white in center), filled star (filled star).\n\nPer-column black cluster detection (like zz_black.py's clusters_at): for each x grid column, find black row clusters. Then classify each cluster.\n\nClassification approach using shape features. Let's compute features for the 4 legend markers (the clean ones) and design a decision rule:\n\nFeatures (within a window around the center, e.g., 13x13):\n1. `topwidth`: width of the topmost black row (within the marker bbox): star is thin (1-2), circle is wide (3-5).\n   - However, for plot markers that have a horizontal line passing through the top edge of the marker, this can break down — a line passing through the top row of the marker adds black pixels. Rare.\n   - Also, overlapping markers: a marker partially hidden by another (e.g., a blue star drawn on top of a black marker, or a red circle overlapping a black star) — the top vertex of the star may be covered! At T=300, blue star ~0.18 and red circle ~0.19 overlap heavily. If the red circle covers the top of a black star, the top row becomes wide → misclassified as a circle. Hmm. But are there black markers under the red/blue at 300 K? The open circle at 300 K is 0.17 (just below the red circle's 0.19 and the blue star's 0.18?) — actually at 300 K: red 0.19, blue 0.18, open circle 0.17 — these three are almost overlapping! And filled circle 0.11, open star 0.09, filled star 0.02.\n\n   Let's actually look at the plot at 300 K (x=101) in pixel detail to see the situation.\n\n2. `center_fill`: the black ratio at the true center (3x3 or 2x2): open circle is low, others are high.\n3. `area`: the total black in the bbox.\n4. White hole features: number/position of white pixels within the bbox.\n\nFor the star: filled vs open — from the legend art:\n- Open star bbox rows 94-101: white pixels inside the star: row 95 col 90 (1px), row 98 cols 88-89? Let's recount from the art (rows 94-101):\n  row94: `...#########...` (cols 84-94? hmm)\n  \nLet's get the exact art for the open star at center (91, 97.5) and the filled star at (91, 115), plus the circles at (91,63.5) and (91,81). Then define features:\n\n- For a star: the white hole of the open star is at the center (the pentagon hole of the star outline), the filled star has no hole at the center (only a notch between the legs at the bottom).\n  → Feature: the white count in the central 5x5: open star > 0, filled star ~0-1.\n  From the art: open star center (91,97 or 98): the central 5x5 includes the row 95 `#######.#######` (1 white) + row 98 `##..#` (2 whites) → about 3 whites. Filled star center (91,115): the central 5x5 rows 113-117: row 113 `.......#.......`? No wait, that's outside the 5x5 (cols 89-93): row 113 col 91='#'; row 114 col 90-91='##'; row 115 `...#########...` col 88-94='#'; row 116 `###############` all; row 117 all. So central 5x5 whites = 0.\n  \n  So: star + central white >= 2 → open; central white <= 1 → filled.\n\n- For a circle: the white hole of the open circle is at the center (a hole of about 3-4px); the filled circle has none.\n  → Central 5x5 whites: open circle ~4-6, filled circle ~0.\n\nSo the classifier:\n1. Determine the bbox from the connected component (or a window).\n2. topwidth (topmost row of the bbox, max width of the top 2 rows): <=3 → star; >=4 → circle.\n   Hmm, but if the marker's top is covered by another marker, or a line runs along the top, the topwidth could be wrong. Fallback: if it looks like a star (width profile), also use the angular variance.\n3. Central white count (5x5 around the bbox center): >=2 → open; <2 → filled.\n\nWait, but for the open circle: topwidth=5 (topmost row `#####`). Filled circle: topmost row `###` (width 3)! From the art: the topmost row of the filled circle `......###......` is width 3, and the topmost row of the open circle `......#####......` is width 5. Uh-oh! So topwidth 3 vs 5 — not cleanly separable. zz_clf used top 2 rows: the top 2 rows of the open circle: row 61 `#####` (5), row 62 `#...##` (3) → max 5. Filled circle: row 78 `###` (3), row 79 `#####` (5) → max 5! So with top-2-rows max, both are 5. Hmm.\n\nOK, another star vs circle discriminator: **the star has vertices** — the width profile w(y) of a star has local minima between the vertex row and the center (indentation), whereas a circle is monotonic up to the middle row.\n\nWidth profile:\n- Open circle (rows 61-66): widths: row61: 5, row62: 3+2=... let's count black pixels per row (a better metric than the span width): row61: 5, row62: 4 (+#..## = 4), row63: 7? `######....#####` — wait, that includes the line stubs at x=80-81 and 101! The legend markers have line stubs extending to the left (x=80-83) and right (x=99-101). These line stubs are at the vertical middle of the marker. So the width is contaminated.\n\nFor markers inside the plot, the connecting line passes horizontally through the marker's center (approximately, since the line connects the centers of consecutive markers, and the slope is shallow; at this scale, the line enters the marker near its vertical center). So the line stubs also pass through the middle row of the plot markers.\n\nThe line is 1-2px thick and passes through the middle, so it affects only the middle rows of the width profile.\n\nStar vs circle: use the top 3 rows:\n- Open circle: row61: 5px (within the circle region x 84-98: `#####` = 5), row62: 4, row63: 4 (left part) + line...\n- Filled circle: row78: 3, row79: 5, row80: 7\n- Open star: row94: 9 (`#########` — the vertex row is 9px wide!), row95: 15 (line+star), row96: 5\n- Filled star: row112: 9, row113: 15, row114: 5\n\nInteresting: the star's vertex row (the row containing the horizontal arms, i.e., about 2px below the top vertex) has a wide width of about 9-11px, whereas the circle never gets wider than about 8px at its top. But at the middle row, the circle is 9-13px (the line stubs).\n\nA better star vs circle discriminator: **the height:width ratio**? Circle: height 6 (61-66), width ~9 → h/w = 0.67. Filled circle: height 7 (78-84), width 9 → 0.78. Star: height 8 (94-101), width 9 → 0.89; filled star height 9 (111-119), width 9 → 1.0. Hmm, the star is taller than it is wide! A circle is wider than it is tall (or equal). But a 5px-diameter circle: the open circle is 6px tall, 5px wide (the circle itself is rows 61-66, cols ~84-98? no wait).\n\nHmm, actually, let's measure the circle's own extent excluding the line stubs. This is getting complicated. Let me take a different, more robust approach:\n\n**Template matching with line tolerance.**\n\nBuild templates from the legend (each 4 types, and if needed, the red circle and blue star too). For each candidate position in the plot, compute a matching score against each template:\n\nscore(t) = (# template black pixels that match plot black) - penalty*(# template white pixels that match plot black) - penalty2*(# template black pixels that don't match plot black)\n\nThe connecting line adds extra black pixels in the plot (in places where the template is white). To tolerate that, make the penalty for template-white/plot-black small (e.g., 0.2 per pixel), and make the penalty for template-black/plot-white larger (1.0), since missing ink is a real mismatch.\n\nFor an open circle, if the plot has a filled circle: the template's central white region (4-6 px)