illed circle ~0.19, open star ~0.16. If I assign both to 0.160, the error is 0 and 0.03. Not great, but not bad.\n\nLet me see if I can do better with shape analysis of the merged blob: within the merged blob, the filled disk is a solid mass (no white inside), and the star has white notches. White pixels inside the blob (rows 194-206, x 118-137): row199 x124-126 (...), row200 x121-127, row202 x129, row204 x125-126, row205 x121-125 & x128-130. Hmm, the star's white notch at row 202 x129 (a gap in the center between the legs) — consistent with the star center at (129, 201), legs at rows 202-205.\n\nLet me try another approach for the whole problem: **for each black series, fit a smooth curve through all the clearly identified points, and predict the low-T values.** zT vs T is a smooth (nearly linear, slightly convex) curve. With 10+ good points, a quadratic fit will give excellent extrapolation to 300–350 K.\n\nFirst, let me identify the clearly resolved points. Scan each T column, get the black clusters, and classify them. Let me write a more complete pipeline:\n\n1. For each T in 300..700, step 25: xc = 100.8+(T-300)/400*226.05.\n2. Black mask; exclude the legend box (x 77-215, y 22-126) — note though that at x 214, y 126 could affect the red region; the red isn't black so no problem. Also exclude the frame rows (y<22 or y>226) and the frame columns (x<77 or x>354).\n3. In the column band xc±2, find clusters of black rows (gap ≤ 2).\n4. For each cluster: estimate the center (weighted average of rows), the row span, and classify the shape using a 13x13 window at (xc, cy):\n   - If the topmost row of the cluster is narrow, it's a star → star.\n   - Count the number of white pixels in the central 3x3/5x5.\n5. Output the classification result.\n\nThen assemble the series, cross-check with the red/blue values, and interpolate the missing parts.\n\nActually, for the stars, there's a cleaner discriminator: **the star has a single-vertex tip at the top: in the topmost row of the marker, there's exactly 1–2 black pixels near the center x, with white on both sides.** For a circle, the topmost row is 3+ pixels. But if the marker is occluded (the tip is hidden), this breaks down.\n\nAnd for the fill state:\n- Open star: a white hole near the center (the star's inner pentagon) — from the legend: a white gap at the center row (row 95 of the legend art, 3px above the bbox center... hmm).\n- Filled star: white only between the two lower legs.\n- Open circle: white hole in the center.\n- Filled circle: no white.\n\nLet me quantify with the legend markers (clean, no lines, no occlusion). Legend markers (x=84-98 is the marker body, x=80-83 and x=99-101 are line stubs):\n\nLet me carefully compute features for each of the four legend markers using a 13x13 window centered on the marker's bbox center, and include:\n- f1: the width (span) of the topmost row of the marker's black pixels.\n- f2: the number of white pixels in the central 3x3.\n- f3: the number of white pixels in the central 5x5.\n- f4: total black area.\n- f5: the number of white pixels in the lower half of the bbox.\n- f6: the number of white pixels in the upper half of the bbox.\n\nLet me compute these."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_485","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/zsh -lc \"cd /home/mato/.cache/real-chart-bench/codex-local/work/pixcal/qwen3.8-27b/part6 && python3 -c \\\"\nimport numpy as np\nfrom PIL import Image\nim=Image.open('images/fig_093.png').convert('RGB')\nA=np.asarray(im).astype(int)\nR,G,B=A[:,:,0],A[:,:,1],A[:,:,2]\nblack=(R<150)&(G<150)&(B<150)\n# legend marker bodies: exclude line stubs x<84 or x>98\ndef feats(cx,cy,name):\n    # 13x13 window\n    win=black[cy-6:cy+7, cx-6:cx+7]\n    white=~win\n    top=win.sum(axis=1)\n    toprows=np.where(top>0)[0]\n    topwidth=top[toprows[0]]\n    top2max=max(top[toprows[0]],top[toprows[1]]) if len(toprows)>1 else top[toprows[0]]\n    c3=white[5:8,5:8].sum()\n    c5=white[4:9,4:9].sum()\n    area=int(win.sum())\n    topwhite=white[:6,:].sum()\n    botwhite=white[7:,:].sum()\n    # width per row profile\n    prof=[int(top[r]) for r in range(13)]\n    print(f'{name:15s} topw={topwidth} top2={top2max} c3w={c3} c5w={c5} area={area} topWh={topwhite} botWh={botwhite} prof={prof}')\nfeats(91,63,'open circle')\nfeats(91,81,'filled circle')\nfeats(91,97,'open star')\nfeats(91,115,'filled star')\n\\\"\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
