{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_658","type":"reasoning","text":"Now I have a good grasp of the overall picture. In the plot area, I can confirm the following 6 curves (from the low-resolution ASCII):\n- Red (R) - filled circle\n- Blue (B) - filled star\n- Black open circles (the topmost black group)\n- Black filled circles\n- Black open stars\n- Black filled stars (bottommost)\n\nLooking at the column scans, in each column (for x>206 to avoid the legend), the following can be identified:\n- red band\n- blue band\n- black bands (4 of them, but some are merged)\n\nLet me try to interpret using the T=650 (x=299) example:\n- red: [65,73] → center 69\n- blue: [73,80] → center 76.5\n- black: [94,100] → open circle, center 97\n- black: [105,112] → filled circle, center 108.5\n- black: [122,128] → open star, center 125\n- black: [166,173] → filled star, center 169.5\n\nThis gives 4 black bands.\n\nBut at T=300 (x=101), the legend overlaps. The legend occupies roughly x 75–205, y 25–120. Actually, the legend markers are at x~91, and the text extends to x~205. Therefore, in columns where x is 101, 115, 129, 143, 157, 171, 186, 200, the black bands in the range y 25–120 are the legend text/markers, not data.\n\nData in these left-side columns is below y~125. Let me try to interpret T=300 (x=101):\n- red: [189,192] center 190.5 (the red at [29,29] is the legend)\n- blue: [191,198] center 194.5 (the blue at [46,46] is the legend)\n- black: [190,191] open circle?, [196,199] filled circle?, [202,221] stars (merged)?\n\nHmm, at T=300, [190,191] and [196,199] are close to red/blue. Let me think about the vertical ordering. In the T=650 column, the ordering (top to bottom) is: red (69), blue (76.5), open circle (97), filled circle (108.5), open star (125), filled star (169.5).\n\nSo, red is at the very top, blue below that, then 4 black ones. But at T=300 (left side, lowest values), the curves converge. Let me check: red 190.5, blue 194.5, then black [190,191], [196,199], [202,221]. So at T=300, the black markers are also at around 190–210, mixed in with red/blue. This is the low-temperature convergence region.\n\nThis is tricky. The black markers and red/blue markers are all close together at low temperatures.\n\nLet me take a cleaner approach. For each column, identify the center of each of the 6 series:\n1. Red: centroid of the red blob (excluding the legend region).\n2. Blue: centroid of the blue blob (excluding the legend region).\n3–6. Black: 4 markers. Need to classify by shape.\n\nFor the black ones, the vertical ordering (top to bottom) is: open circle, filled circle, open star, filled star. If the bands can be separated, this ordering should hold in every column. But in some columns, the bands get merged (e.g., the [202,221] at T=300 is 2 stars + a line merged together).\n\nLet me use shape classification to robustly identify each black marker. I'll use the following features:\n- Open circle: has an enclosed white hole (ring). Hole area ~3–10.\n- Filled circle: solid, no hole, roundish, width ~7–9, height ~7–9.\n- Open star: has an enclosed white hole (star-shaped interior), star-shaped.\n- Filled star: solid, no hole, star-shaped, height ~9–11, with pointed top and bottom.\n\nMain distinguishing features:\n- Hole presence: open circle (has hole), filled circle (no hole), open star (has hole), filled star (no hole).\n- Star shape: pointed vertical extent (top tip + bottom tip), wider horizontal bar.\n- Circle: round, no points.\n\nSo:\n- Has hole + round → open circle\n- No hole + round → filled circle\n- Has hole + star → open star\n- No hole + star → filled star\n\nFor star vs. circle: stars have a characteristic top tip (narrow top) and pointed bottom, with a wide horizontal bar. Circles are round.\n\nLet me write a robust detector:\n1. Find all black connected components in the plot area (excluding the legend + frame + tick labels).\n2. But the black markers are connected to the black connecting lines! So the components are large (line + all markers in a series connected together). This is the problem — the lines connect the markers.\n\nSo I can't just use connected components. Instead, I need to work column by column.\n\nApproach: in each temperature column (x=xc), extract the black pixels in the vertical strip [xc-3, xc+3]. Group them into bands (contiguous in the vertical direction). Then, for each band, classify the shape using the 2D pattern within a small window around the band center.\n\nBut when markers overlap with lines and each other, the bands can be large. Let me handle each band:\n- Band height ~2–3 px: probably part of an open circle's ring (top or bottom) or an open star's edge, or a line.\n- Band height ~7–9 px, round: filled circle or filled star.\n- Band height ~9–11 px, star-shaped: filled star.\n\nActually, the cleanest thing is: use the vertical ordering. In each column, the 4 black markers are ordered (top to bottom) as: open circle, filled circle, open star, filled star. So if I can find 4 black \"marker centers\" in each column, I just need to assign them in order.\n\nHow to find the 4 centers? Use the local density peak method from f93_final.py (dens_peaks). That worked reasonably well. Let me rerun a refined version and inspect the results.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Let me look at the data more carefully. Let me plot the exact positions. Let me use a robust method:\n\nFor each column xc (17 columns), do the following:\n- Red center: centroid of red pixels in [xc-4, xc+4], y in [136..225] (below the legend), or more generally y<228. Actually, the red data spans y~65..195. To avoid the legend, restrict to y>130 for the left columns... no, that's not right. For columns where x>206, the legend is at x<206, so no conflict. For columns with x<206, the red legend marker is at y~29, x~91. The red data is at y~190+ for left columns. So just take the red band (data) and use its centroid. There's only one red band (the legend red is at x~91, a different column). Good — since the red legend marker is only at x~91 (the legend column), and the data columns are specific x values, there's no overlap. Actually, at x=101 (T=300), the red band [189,192] is the data, and the red at [29,29] in that column — wait, is [29,29] the legend? The legend marker is at x~91, but the legend line extends. Let me check: in the earlier full column scan at T=300 (x=101), red: [29,29] and [189,192]. The [29,29] is the legend's red line/marker extending to x=101. So I need to exclude the legend region for the left columns.\n\nSo the rule is: exclude pixels with (x<206 and y<136) [legend region]. Then the red data is [189,192] at T=300. Good.\n\nLet me write a clean, comprehensive script that does the following:\n1. For each of the 17 columns xc (T=300..700, step 25):\n   a. Red: centroid of red pixels in the column window, excluding the legend.\n   b. Blue: centroid of blue pixels in the column window, excluding the legend.\n   c. Black: find the 4 marker centers.\n\nFor the black markers, use the following approach:\n- Extract the black strip in the column window, excluding the legend and frame (y between 22 and 226, and excluding the legend box).\n- Find \"marker centers\" using local density: a marker is where black pixels are locally dense (a blob), in contrast to a thin line.\n- Use a 2D sliding window that scores how \"blob-like\" the region is.\n\nActually, let me use the dens_peaks method that f93_final.py uses, and tune the threshold. Let me rerun f93_final.py to check its output."}}
