do this carefully with a proper script that classifies each marker by shape and fill, then assigns them to series. The shape classification (circle vs star, open vs filled) is the most robust approach, since it doesn't depend on y-ordering.\n\nLet me write a comprehensive marker-classification script.\n\nFor black markers:\n- Open vs filled: look at a small window around the marker center. If there's an enclosed white region in the middle → open. If solid black → filled.\n- Circle vs star:\n  - Circle: roughly circular blob, aspect ratio ~1, no sharp points\n  - Star: 5-pointed, wider bounding box relative to fill, points sticking out\n\nHow to distinguish a circle from a star:\n- Filled circle: solid disk. Bounding box is about as wide as it is tall, fill ratio (black pixels / bbox area) is high (~π/4 = 0.785 for a circle).\n- Filled star: has points. Fill ratio is lower (~0.3-0.4), aspect ratio... a 5-pointed star is about as wide as it is tall.\n- So for filled markers, fill ratio is a good discriminator: circle ~0.7-0.8, star ~0.35-0.45.\n\nFor open markers:\n- Open circle: ring.\n- Open star: star-shaped outline.\n- The holes inside: the hole of an open circle is roughly circular; the hole of an open star is star-shaped (pentagon-like).\n\nActually, a simpler and more robust method: for each marker, compute the fill ratio of the black region within the bounding box.\n- Filled circle: high (~0.75)\n- Filled star: low (~0.4)\n- Open circle: low (only the ring is filled, ~0.4-0.5)\n- Open star: low (~0.3)\n\nHmm, open markers and filled stars might have similar fill ratios. So I need to combine open/filled determination with shape.\n\nLet me do this in 2 steps:\n1. Open vs filled: check whether the center pixel region is white (open) or black (filled).\n2. Circle vs star: use fill ratio and shape.\n\nActually, the most reliable is:\n- Determine open vs filled by whether there's an enclosed white hole.\n- Determine circle vs star by shape: stars have corners/points, circles are smooth. Use the fill ratio of the black pixels within the marker's bbox:\n  - Filled circle: ~0.75\n  - Filled star: ~0.42\n  - Open circle: ~0.45 (ring)\n  - Open star: ~0.30\n\nCombining:\n- Filled + high ratio → filled circle (EuCd2Sb2)\n- Filled + low ratio → filled star (YbZn2Sb2)\n- Open + medium-to-high ratio → open circle (EuZn2Sb2)\n- Open + low ratio → open star (YbCd2Sb2)\n\nThis should work well. Let me implement it.\n\nBut there's a complication: markers connected by lines. The bbox might include the connecting line, messing up the fill ratio. I need to isolate each marker.\n\nApproach:\n1. Create the black mask (exclude frame and legend).\n2. Detect marker centers. For filled markers: erosion → cores. For open markers: detect holes (enclosed white regions) → hole center ≈ marker center.\n3. For each marker center, extract a clean window (e.g., 9x9 or 11x11) around it, and within that window compute the black/white pattern to classify.\n\nBut the lines will pass through the window. Hmm.\n\nAlternative: use morphology to separate markers from lines. Markers are about 5-7px wide, lines are 1-2px. Erode with a disk of radius 2: this removes lines (1-2px) and also removes the thin rings of open markers, leaving the cores of filled markers. That gives the filled markers. For the open markers, the rings are thin so erosion removes them too — but I can detect them via the holes.\n\nSo:\n- Filled markers (filled circle + filled star): found by erosion cores.\n- Open markers (open circle + open star): found by enclosed white holes.\n\nThen classify each by shape.\n\nFor filled markers (from erosion cores), I know the center. To distinguish circle from star, look at the original (un-eroded) black region around the center:\n- Filled circle: the black region around the center is a disk.\n- Filled star: the black region is a star with points.\nCompute the number of black pixels within a radius-R disk around the center, and the extent (max radius) in each angular direction. A star has points (max radius in 5 directions) with valleys between them. A circle has a nearly constant radius.\n\nA simpler, more robust metric: within a window around the center, fill ratio = black pixels / window area. For a 13x13 window:\n- Filled circle (radius ~3): area ~28, but the window is 169, and the disk fills ~28/169... no, this depends on the window size.\n\nLet me use a better approach: for each marker, compute the black region's own bbox (isolating it from the line). To isolate it, take the connected component of the black region that contains the marker, but that includes the line...\n\nHmm, this is the fundamental problem: the lines connect the markers.\n\nLet me think differently. I'll use a fixed-size analysis window around each detected center and compute:\n1. Is it open or filled: check the center 3x3 — if mostly white and surrounded by black → open; if black → filled.\n2. For shape: look at the pattern.\n\nActually, I have a cleaner idea. Let me use the hole shape to directly distinguish the open circle from the open star (they're white holes):\n- Open circle hole: roughly circular, high fill ratio within its bbox.\n- Open star hole: star-shaped, lower fill ratio, and the hole is smaller.\n\nAnd for the filled markers, use the fill ratio of the black core.\n\nBut I still need to handle the line contamination in the bbox.\n\nLet me step back and think about the most practical, robust approach given the small image size (405x300) and small markers.\n\nPractical approach:\n1. Get the x positions of the ~17 data points (from red/blue detection: x ≈ 100, 114, 128, 142, 157, 171, 185, 199, 213, 228, 241, 256, 270, 284, 298, 312, 326).\n2. For each x column, look at the vertical strip (x±3) and find all black marker blobs in the plot area.\n3. For each black blob, get its center y and classify its shape (open/filled, circle/star).\n4. Assign to the 4 black series.\n\nFor classification, I'll extract a window around each blob and use template-like features. Let me implement and iterate.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Let me reconsider using fill ratio on the isolated blob. To isolate a marker from the line, I can use this trick: the line is thin (1-2px), the marker is thick (5-7px). So if I erode the black mask with a 3x3 (or 2x2) kernel, the line disappears and the marker shrinks but remains. Then dilate back. This isolates the marker (removing the line). But open markers (thin rings) will disappear under erosion. So:\n\n- For filled markers: erode → dilate → filled markers without lines. Then classify circle vs star by shape.\n- For open markers: the ring is ~1-2px, so it also disappears under erosion. Hmm.\n\nFor open markers, the ring is thin. But I can detect them via the enclosed white hole (the interior). The hole is a clean shape (circle or star). So:\n- Open circle: hole is circular.\n- Open star: hole is star-shaped.\n\nAnd I can get the center of the marker from the center of the hole.\n\nSo the plan:\n- Filled markers: isolated by erode+dilate. Classify by fill ratio/shape.\n- Open markers: find enclosed white holes, classify hole shape (circle vs star), center = hole center.\n\nLet me implement this. It's a solid approach.\n\nLet me write the script.\n\nFor circle vs star classification of a blob (black blob for filled, white hole for open):\n- Compute the bbox (w x h).\n- Fill ratio = area / (w*h).\n  - Circle: ~0.78 (π/4)\n  - Star: ~0.3-0.45\n- Also, a star's bbox is roughly square, and a circle's bbox is roughly square. So aspect ratio doesn't help much. Fill ratio is the key.\n- Threshold: fill ratio > 0.6 → circle; < 0.6 → star.\n\nLet me verify with the actual markers. Let me write the script and print out the fill ratios to calibrate the threshold.\n\nLet me do it."}}
