{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_798","type":"reasoning","text":"The hole list is noisy — it includes the \"gap\" between the line and the marker (the region enclosed by the line and the marker), not just marker holes. For example, (83.7, 26.0) is outside the plot (x=83 < 78? no, 83.7 > 78… that's the x-axis label region? y=26, x=83 — that's to the left of the plot's left frame ~x=95? hmm, x=83.7 is outside the plot area (the left frame is at x≈95?). Wait, my filter was 78<cx<350. The plot's left frame is… the x tick 300K is at px 100.8. The frame's left edge is probably ~95. So (83.7, 26) is outside the plot — inside the y-axis label region! It must be a hole inside the digit \"1.4\" of the y-axis label. Damn — my x filter 78 is too loose.\n\nAlso, many of the \"holes\" are actually the enclosed gap between a curve's line and the marker — e.g., between consecutive points on the red curve? No wait, red is colored and not black. The gaps enclosed between the black curve and the colored curve…\n\nHmm, actually the \"hole\" = a white region completely surrounded by black (or non-white). When the red curve and the black curve run parallel, the region between them is enclosed by both, and the white gap between the black line and the red line can become a \"hole\" in the white mask (surrounded by black+red = non-white). That's the source of the noise: e.g., (122.5, 185.3) T=338 — that's the gap between the blue curve (upper) and the black curve (lower)? At T~338, blue is ZT 0.25 → y≈186; the black filled-circle curve is ~0.18 → y≈197. The gap between them is enclosed by blue (upper) and black (lower) lines → a white hole. Yes! That explains the \"hole\" positioned between two curves.\n\nTherefore, hole detection must be restricted to holes \"surrounded by black\" (all non-white adjacent pixels are black), not by colored lines. Better approach: compute holes in the black mask (not the white mask): i.e., fill the holes of the black mask and find the new pixels. Those are the holes enclosed by black.\n\nLet's do that:\n- black_mask → filled = ndimage.binary_fill_holes(black). holes = filled & ~black.\n- Exclude frame region and legend.\n- Label the holes, keep those with area 3-60, get centroid, and classify as circular/star-shaped.\n\nThe interior of an open marker is surrounded only by black (the ring). Gaps between the black curve and the colored curve are not enclosed by black alone (the boundary is partially colored), so they won't appear as holes in the black mask.\n\nBut note: when an open marker's ring touches/overlaps with a line, the hole may merge with the exterior (the ring is broken) → that hole is lost. And when two open markers overlap (e.g., open circle and open star are adjacent), the holes could merge.\n\nLet's run this improved detection. Also, I'll look at the zoomed image I created to check the dense zone."}}
