pen-marker finder:\n\nFor each grid x column (17 columns), consider a strip x±5. In that strip, classify each row into: white/black/red/blue. Then find \"open marker\" patterns:\n- Open circle: black ring above and below (and on both sides) with white in the center. In the strip: a pattern like black (rows y0..y0+2), white (y0+3..y1-3), black (y1-2..y1), with height ~6-8 px.\n- Open star: similar but with the arms.\n\nBut in the strip, lines also cross through, complicating the pattern.\n\nAlternative: template matching via normalized cross-correlation against an ideal ring template, using the \"dark\" mask (black + red + blue, i.e., non-white). Open circle: ring of dark pixels at radius 3-4, center white. Define the score at (cx,cy):\n  center_white = fraction of white pixels in the disk of radius 1.5 ≥ 0.5\n  ring_dark = fraction of non-white in the annulus of radius 2.5-4.5 ≥ 0.7\nFor open star: arms extend to radius 5-6; ring_dark at radius 2.5-4.5 would be lower (the arms are sparse), the center white disk is smaller.\n\nHmm. Let me try it anyway and see what shows up. For all candidates (x = grid columns ±1, y in 20..226), score = ring_dark * center_white, and see if the open markers stand out.\n\nActually, let me think about what distinguishes an open circle from a filled circle in this strip: a filled circle has a black center. An open circle has a white center. Both have a round dark perimeter. So: for each grid column, in the strip, find all round dark blobs of diameter 5-9 px (via labeling on the dark mask), and for each, compute the center color: white → open circle; black → filled circle.\n\nThis is clean! Similarly, the arms of an open star + a white center vs. a filled star's black center.\n\nSo: build the dark mask (non-white = black + red + blue, after clearing the frame and legend). Label the blobs with area 15-80 px and bounding box 5-11 px in both dimensions. For each blob, center point (centroid): sample the color: if white → open; if black → filled. Then shape: circle vs star — from the fill ratio of the blob in its bbox: circle ~0.7-0.8, star ~0.4-0.6.\n\nBut wait: blobs will include the line + marker merged (the line pierces the marker, so blob = marker + line segment, area larger, bbox elongated). E.g., filled circle + line: bbox ~7x9. That's OK, as long as I keep the size bounds.\n\nHmm, but at 300 K, the red circle + blue star overlap → the dark blob is merged. And at low T, the black markers are densely packed (series A, open star, series B within ~15 px) → the blobs may merge with each other via the lines.\n\nOK, let me step back and be pragmatic. What I need is:\n1. Red: 15 points. I have 15 blobs, but the first few (329, 346, 350, 354) are fragments — the 325 K and 350 K markers are split (probably by blue overlap), and the 300 K marker is completely missing (covered by the blue star). Let me find the red centers with a better method: for the red circle at grid x 100.8, 114.9, 128.9: take all red pixels within x±6 of the grid x, and compute the median y? But the red line also passes through... the line has red pixels at all y along the way. The marker's bulge: red pixels are most concentrated at the marker's y. I can take a red pixel histogram in the strip and find the y with maximum local density. Or, since I know red is roughly monotonic, interpolate: red at 300 ≈ between blue (0.192) and the red trend. From the overall view, red at 300 K ≈ 0.20-0.21 (just above blue). Let me measure: the red pixels near x=100.8: find the y-range.\n\n2. Blue: 17 clean points. ✓ But wait — is the blue at 300 K biased by the red circle above it? Blue blob (100.7, 194.9) ZT=0.192. In the c300 zoom, the blue star is clearly visible at px~100, y~187-195. Its center is ~192? The centroid is 194.9 — maybe biased low by the blue line going toward the lower-left. Hmm, a ±2 px bias → 0.014 ZT. Acceptable, but let me double-check with the star's arms.\n\n3. Black filled circle (EuCd2Sb2): 15 points from erosion. ✓ (ZT 0.122→0.673)\n4. Black filled star (YbZn2Sb2): 17 points from erosion. ✓ (ZT 0.035→0.464)\n5. Open star (YbCd2Sb2): need 17 points; have 300 (0.091), 625 (0.705), 650 (0.790); from the overall view, 700 (0.98).\n6. Open circle (EuZn2Sb2): need 15 points; have 625 (0.851), 650 (0.868).\n\nWait, actually — hold on. Let me reconsider which is which. Legend:\n1. EuZn1.8Cd0.2Sb2 — red filled circle\n2. YbCd1.6Zn0.4Sb2 — blue filled star\n3. EuZn2Sb2 — black open circle\n4. EuCd2Sb2 — black filled circle\n5. YbCd2Sb2 — black open star\n6. YbZn2Sb2 — black filled star\n\nFrom the chemistry (Eu vs Yb, Zn vs Cd), the order at high T is: YbCd1.6Zn0.4Sb2 (blue, highest), EuZn1.8Cd0.2Sb2 (red), then EuZn2Sb2 (open circle), YbCd2Sb2 (open star), EuCd2Sb2 (filled circle), YbZn2Sb2 (filled star, lowest).\n\nAt 625 K: open circle 0.851 > open star 0.705 > filled circle 0.616 > filled star 0.325. So open circle (EuZn2Sb2) is above open star (YbCd2Sb2), which is above filled circle (EuCd2Sb2). ✓ Consistent.\n\nSo at 300 K: open circle is above 0.122 (filled circle)? and open star 0.091, filled star 0.035. Where is the open circle at 300? From the band analysis, the open circle is ~8 px above series A: at 325 K, series A y=201.4 → open circle y≈193-195 (ZT ~0.20)? Hmm, but in the overall view, at 325 K I see: red 0.25, blue 0.22, and black... the c300 crop shows px 114 (T=325): a large filled blob at y~201-210 (series A, filled circle), a faint line at y~216 (series B, filled star), and the open star at 325 K is?? And the open circle at 325 K is??\n\nWait, in the c300 crop, at px~114-128, I don't see a separate open star or open circle — just one large filled blob (which I thought was a filled circle) and the filled-star line. But wait — that large blob at (114, 205): could it be an open circle + filled circle merged (overlapping), looking like one large filled blob? The blob's sz=12 after erosion (erosion leaves the core) — if it were two overlapping circles, the erosion core would be larger.\n\nHmm, let me look at the c300 crop again... at px~114: the blob spans y~199-211, width ~10 px. A filled circle at 325 K is ~7 px diameter. A 10-px wide, 12-px tall blob could be a filled circle + open circle overlap + the line.\n\nUgh. OK. Time for a definitive approach: for each grid column, do a careful pixel-level classification in the strip, and display an ASCII rendering of the strip so I can visually read off the marker positions. That was the zz_cols.py approach. Let me render each grid column's strip (x±5, y 16-226) as ASCII with character classes R/B/K/./o, and read the structure by eye. With 17 columns, I can display them all in one image.\n\nLet's do that."}}
