white, dark? No — vertically through the center: top edge dark (1–2 px), then white interior (1–3 px), then bottom edge dark (1–2 px). So in the strip there's a 4–5 px pattern with a white middle.\n- Star: a 5-pointed star has a dense center and spikes.\n\nAlternatively, the previous agent's approach: use a feature profile (annulus ratio, etc.) to scan y at each grid x. That's complex; a simpler per-column approach may be more robust:\n\nFor each grid column cx, for each y from top to bottom, compute the \"thickness\" of each color within the window [cx-3, cx+3] × [y-3, y+3]:\n- Red thickness = number of red pixels in the 7x7 window. Red circle: many red pixels (>15). Red line: few (<8).\n- Blue similar.\n- Black: total black pixels in the window minus those belonging to lines... Black filled circle: a black blob of ~20+ px in the 7x7 window. Open circle: a ring of ~12–18 px (with a white center). Star: ~15–20 px. Black line: ~7 px (1–2 px thick, 7 px wide ≈ 7–14 px).\n\nThen, local maxima in y give marker candidates. Then assign candidates to series based on vertical order and shape.\n\nBut the 3 black series: at a given x, their y positions are distinct (except where they cross?). From the big image: open circle (EuZn2Sb2) is above filled circle (EuCd2Sb2), which is above open star (YbCd2Sb2)?? Wait no. Let me look at the big image again.\n\nFrom the 8x big image, from top to bottom at high T:\n1. Red circle (top, ends at 650)\n2. Blue star (highest at 700)\n3. Open circle — below the blue star in the mid-range; at 650–700, the open circle is ~0.81–0.88, just below the blue star\n4. Black filled circle — 0.57 at 650\n5. Black star (open star) — 0.79 at 650\n6. Black filled star — bottom, 0.36 at 650\n\nWait, that ordering is: at 650 K: red 1.06, blue 1.03, open circle 0.81, open star 0.79?? And filled circle 0.57, filled star 0.36. Hmm, so the open star (YbCd2Sb2) is above the filled circle (EuCd2Sb2) at 650 K? In the image, in the mid-region I see 2 black series 