{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_853","type":"reasoning","text":"Now the right-side region (x 230-345 px, i.e., about 545 K to 700 K) is clearly visible:\n\n- **Red filled circles**: at the rightmost end, the last red circle is near x≈300 (about 650 K) — I can see the red circles stepping up to the upper right. In this crop the last red circle is around x=300 (650 K). So the red series ends at 650 K.\n- **Blue stars**: continues up to 700 K (topmost, about 1.23).\n- **Open circles (EuZn2Sb2)**: in this crop the last open circle is around x≈313? I can see two open circles in the upper region: one near x≈299 (650 K, y~0.81), and one near x≈313 (675 K, y~0.86). So the open circles end at 675 K.\n- **Black open stars (YbCd2Sb2)**: the last one is around x≈327 (700 K), at y~0.96.\n- **Black filled circles (EuCd2Sb2)**: the last black filled circle in this crop is around x≈313 (675 K, y~0.68).\n- **Black filled stars (YbZn2Sb2)**: continues to 700 K (y~0.46).\n\nFrom the zoom, at the far right (700 K): blue star 1.23, open star 0.96, filled star 0.47. At 675 K: open circle 0.86, filled circle 0.68. So:\n\n- Red circles: 300→650 (13 points)\n- Blue stars: 300→700 (17 points)\n- Open circles: 300→675 (15 points)\n- Filled circles: 300→675 (15 points)\n- Open stars: 300→700 (17 points)\n- Filled stars: 300→700 (17 points)\n\nWait, I need to re-check the leftmost endpoint: does every series start at 300 K? From the full image: yes, they all start at 300 K.\n\nAlso, I need to verify the legend marker style of each series (legend order):\n1. EuZn1.8Cd0.2Sb2 — red filled circle\n2. YbCd1.6Zn0.4Sb2 — blue star\n3. EuZn2Sb2 — open circle\n4. EuCd2Sb2 — black filled circle\n5. YbCd2Sb2 — black open star (outline)\n6. YbZn2Sb2 — black filled star\n\nFrom the legend crop (zz93_leg8.png), the markers were cut off (the crop started at x=110, which included only the text; the markers are at x~115-125?). Actually in the first legend zoom (zz93_leg10x.png), the markers were visible: red circle, blue star, open circle, black filled circle, black open star, black filled star. That matches.\n\nWait, but in the first legend zoom, row 4 (EuCd2Sb2) was a black filled circle, row 5 (YbCd2Sb2) was a black open star (with white center), and row 6 (YbZn2Sb2) was a black filled star. Let me zoom in again to double-check rows 4-6 of the legend… From zz93_leg10x.png:\n- EuZn1.8Cd0.2Sb2: red circle ✓\n- YbCd1.6Zn0.4Sb2: blue star ✓\n- EuZn2Sb2: open circle (white center, black outline) ✓\n- EuCd2Sb2: black filled circle ✓\n- YbCd2Sb2: black star outline (open star) ✓\n- YbZn2Sb2: black filled star ✓\n\nOkay, the tricky part is distinguishing the black open circles from the black filled circles, and the black open stars from the black filled stars.\n\nBlack filled circle: a solid black disk, about 5-6 px in diameter.\nBlack open circle: a ring — a black outline with a white center, about 5-6 px in diameter.\nBlack filled star: a solid black star.\nBlack open star: a star outline with a white/gray center.\n\nIn the right-side zoom, the open circle is clearly visible (a white center). The open star is… near the top-right, around 675-700 K I can see an open star (white center, star outline). The filled star is the bottommost curve, and it's solid black.\n\nSo the 6 series are:\n1. Red filled circles (EuZn1.8Cd0.2Sb2)\n2. Blue stars (YbCd1.6Zn0.4Sb2)\n3. Black open circles (EuZn2Sb2)\n4. Black filled circles (EuCd2Sb2)\n5. Black open stars (YbCd2Sb2)\n6. Black filled stars (YbZn2Sb2)\n\nNow, the extraction strategy:\n\nx grid: T = 300, 325, ..., 700 (17 columns). px = 100.8 + (T-300)*(326.85-100.8)/400 = 100.8 + (T-300)*0.61515625.\n\nFor each column, detect the markers:\n\n1. Red: red mask (r>140, g<110, b<110). In a window of cx±4, find y clusters where the red pixel count is high. Red circle markers: red blobs about 5 px wide. Lines are also red but thin. Use a sliding window: for each y, count red pixels in [cx-3,cx+3]x[y-3,y+3]; the marker center is where this count is maximal (about 20+ px vs about 10 for a line crossing). Then refine the center by a local weighted centroid of the red pixels near (cx, cy).\n\n2. Blue: similarly with the blue mask.\n\n3. Black open circles: black ring with a white center. Detect a black cluster in the column window; for each black y cluster (a contiguous run of black rows in the central column region), check: height 3-6 px, and the center pixel is white.\n\n4. Black filled circles: black cluster, height 3-6 px, center is black.\n\n5. Black open stars: star-shaped outline; the vertical extent is about 5-7 px; the center region is partially white. Star shape: at the center x, the topmost spike is 1 px, then wider. The white center may be partially filled by lines passing through.\n\n6. Black filled stars: a solid black star, vertical extent 5-7 px, dense.\n\nSince multiple black series can have different y values in the same column, find all black clusters in the column window and classify each by shape.\n\nAlso, note that lines connecting markers are also black (open circle series, filled circle series, star series) — the lines pass through the column window between markers and create small black runs (1-2 px). A marker cluster is taller (3-7 px). So I'll filter by height.\n\nBut there's a subtle point: in a column exactly at the marker x, the vertical line through the center of a filled circle has height about 5-6 px; an open circle has height about 5-6 px with a white center; a star has height about 5-7 px. A line crossing the window diagonally is about 1-3 px tall.\n\nAlso, open circle and filled circle markers might overlap with nearby series (e.g., open circle near the blue star line).\n\nLet me write a general-purpose detection: for each grid column cx, extract a vertical strip of width 9 px (cx-4..cx+4), y range 20..222 (within the plot). Find connected components of black pixels in the strip? A better way: cluster rows: for each row y, count the black pixels in the strip; 