13.7). I'll grab the black clusters there and classify their shapes.\n\nLet me write a shape classifier. For a marker centered at (cx, cy), extract a 13x13 window and compute features:\n- Total black pixel count\n- Center pixel (is it black or white/gray?) → filled or open\n- Shape: circle or star → use the ratio of horizontal width at the \"waist\" to the maximum width, or count the number of \"arms\"\n\nFor circle vs star:\n- Circle: a ring (open) or disk (filled). Cross-section at the center row: wide (the entire diameter).\n- Star: has a pointed top, a horizontal bar in the middle, and pointed legs. Cross-section at the center row: wide (the bar), but the top rows are narrow (a single point).\n\nFeatures to distinguish circle vs star:\n- Filled circle (from the legend):\n  ```\n  .....###.......\n  ....#####......\n  ...#######.....\n  ################\n  ....######.....\n  ....#####......\n  ```\n  Width profile (number of black pixels per row, excluding the line): 3, 5, 7, 16 (line), 6, 5. So a rounded blob, width grows 3 → 7 and then shrinks. Max width around the center.\n\n- Filled star (from the legend):\n  ```\n  .......#.......\n  .......##......\n  ...#########...\n  ################\n  ooooo#####oooooo\n  .....#####......\n  .....##.##......\n  .....o....#.....\n  ```\n  Width profile: 1, 2, 9, 16 (line), 5, 5, 2 + gaps. Top is a single point (width 1), then widens to 9, then the bar (16), then legs.\n\nKey difference: the top row of a star is narrow (1-2 px, a single point), whereas the top row of a circle is wider (3 px). Also, a star has a clear point at the top center.\n\nLet me define: for a marker, look at the rows above center. For a circle, the topmost row is around 3-4 px wide and centered. For a star, the topmost row is 1 px (a single point).\n\nActually, the most robust discriminator between a filled circle and a filled star is: the \"pointiness\" at the top. Let me use: in the 3 rows above the center, is there a single column that sticks up higher than the rest (the star's top point)?\n\nFor a star: the top point is at the center column, extending up 2-3 rows, while the sides are low.\nFor a circle: the top is a smooth arc, and the topmost point is also at the center, but the width grows gradually.\n\nHmm, both have their topmost point at the center. The difference is the growth rate: a star's top is very pointy (width 1 at the top, then 2, then jumps to 9), whereas a circle's top is rounded (width 3, then 5, then 7).\n\nLet me use the width of the topmost row: circle ~ 3-4 px, star ~ 1-2 px. And the width at the row 2 rows above center: circle ~ 5, star ~ 2.\n\nFor open vs filled: check the center pixel. Circle center: open → white; filled → black. Star center: open → white/gray; filled → black.\n\nLet me build the classifier and test it on the legend markers, then apply it to all black markers in the plot.\n\nLet me define the classifier:\n```\ndef classify_marker(blk, cx, cy):\n    # blk: black mask\n    # Returns (open/filled, circle/star)\n    # Window\n    y0,y1 = cy-6, cy+7\n    x0,x1 = cx-7, cx+7\n    # center pixel\n    center = blk[cy, cx]\n    filled = center  # True if center is black\n    # top pointiness: width of topmost row\n    # find the topmost row with black in this window (excluding the horizontal line)\n    ...\n```\n\nThis is getting complicated because of the horizontal line running through the marker. The line adds a full-width row. Let me exclude the line.\n\nActually, the line is horizontal at y = cy (the marker center). In the legend, the line is at y = 81 (filled circle), y = 98 (open star), etc. — exactly the center row. So the center row is dominated by the line.\n\nLet me work with rows other than the center. Let me look at the shape in the upper half (rows cy-6 to cy-1) and lower half (rows cy+1 to cy+6).\n\nFor a star: upper half = a single point at the top center, then a bar. The top point is 1 px wide at the very top.\nFor a circle: upper half = a smooth arc.\n\nLet me measure: within the upper half, the width of the topmost row (the first row from the top that contains a marker pixel, i.e., excluding the line).\n- Star: topmost row width = 1 (a single point).\n- Circle: topmost row width = 3 or more.\n\nAnd the center: filled vs open.\n\nLet me test this classifier on the 4 legend markers.\n\nFrom the legend data:\n- Open circle (y = 63):\n  ```\n  61 .....o####......\n  62 .....#...##.....\n  63 ######....######   <- center row (line), center is white (open)\n  64 oooo##...o#ooooo\n  65 .....#...#o.....\n  66 ......####......\n  ```\n  Topmost marker row = 61, the \"o####\" part is at x ~ 89-92 (width ~ 4, but includes the gray o). The actual top arc: rows 61-62. Center (63, ~ 91) is white → open. Shape: circle (rounded, hollow).\n- Filled circle (y = 81):\n  ```\n  78 ......###.......\n  79 .....#####......\n  80 ....#######.....\n  81 ################  <- center (line), center is black (filled)\n  82 .....######.....\n  83 .....#####......\n  ```\n  Topmost row 78 width 3. Center is black → filled. Circle.\n- Open star (y = 98):\n  ```\n  94 .......#........\n  95 .......#........\n  96 .......##.......\n  97 ...#########....\n  98 #######.########  <- center (line), center: (98, ~ 91) = '.'? Let me check. Row 98: \"#######.########\" starting at x = 84. x = 84-90 = #######, x = 91 = '.', x = 92-99 = ########. So center (98, 91) = '.' white → open.\n  99 .....o####......\n  100 .....#####......\n  101 .....#o..#......\n  ```\n  Topmost row 94 width 1 (a single point) → star. Center is white → open.\n- Filled star (y = 115):\n  ```\n  111 .......o........\n  112 .......#........\n  113 .......##.......\n  114 ...#########....\n  115 ################  <- center (line), center (115, 91) = '#'? Row 115 starting at x = 84: \"################\" all 16 = #. So center is black → filled.\n  116 ooooo#####oooooo\n  117 .....#####......\n  118 .....##.##......\n  119 .....o....#.....\n  ```\n  Topmost row 112 width 1 (point) → star. Center is black → filled.\n\nSo the classifier:\n- Star vs circle: width of the topmost row (excluding the center line row). Star = 1-2, circle = 3 or more.\n- Open vs filled: color of the center pixel.\n\nLet me formalize. For a marker centered at (cx, cy):\n- Top region: rows cy-4 to cy-1 (4 rows above center), columns cx-6 to cx+6.\n- Find the topmost row t that has a marker pixel (black) in this region (excluding the line).\n- Width of row t = number of black pixels.\n- If width is 2 or less → star; if 3 or more → circle.\n\nWait, but for an open circle, the top arc at row 61 is \"o####\" = gray + 4 black. Width of the topmost row (61): the black pixels at x ~ 89-92 (4 px) plus gray. Hmm, but that's the top of the ring. Let me reconsider — for an open circle, the topmost row is the outer edge of the ring. For a filled circle, the topmost row is also the top of the disk. Both are around 3-4 px. For a star, the topmost row is a single point (1 px).\n\nBut there's ambiguity: the topmost row of an open circle could be 3 or 4. Let me use a threshold: width is 2 or less → star, otherwise → circle.\n\nActually, let me use a more robust metric: the \"height of the top point.\" For a star, the top point extends higher relative to the body. Let me use the ratio: (width of the topmost row) / (width of the widest non-line row).\n- Circle: top/widest ~ 3/7 ~ 0.4 or more (the top arc is a substantial fraction of the width).\n- Star: top/widest ~ 1/9 ~ 0.11 (a narrow point relative to the bar).\n\nHmm, but the widest row of a circle is the center (the line row), which I'm excluding. Let me use the widest non-line row. For a circle, the widest non-line row is around the center ~ 7. The top row is 3-4. Ratio ~ 0.5.\nFor a star, the widest non-line row is the bar ~ 9. The top point is 1. Ratio ~ 0.11.\n\nSo the threshold: ratio is 0.3 or more → circle, otherwise → star. Let me test this.\n\nActually, let me use an even simpler and more robust approach. Let me count the black pixels in a small vertical strip at the center column (cx-1, cx, cx+1) in the top region (cy-5 to cy-1).\n- Star: the top point is at the center, so this strip is filled for 2-3 rows (the point) and then empty (the indent) until the bar. Actually, the top point of the star is at the center: rows cy-5 (o, gray), cy-4 (#), cy-3 (##)... let me see. Filled star: row 111 (o, center x = 91), 112 (#, x = 91), 113 (##, x = 91-92), 114 (bar). So the center column is black at rows 112, 113, 114.\n- Circle: the top arc at the center: filled circle rows 78 (###, including x = 91), 79, 80, 81 (line). So the center column is black at rows 78, 79, 80.\n\nBoth are filled at the center. Not a good discriminator.\n\nLet me go with the topmost-row-width approach. Let me implement it and test it on the legend.\n\nActually, I realize there might be a cleaner approach for the whole problem. Since I know the x positions of the markers (T = 300 to 700 in steps of 25), and there are 4 black curves, let me do the following:\n\n1. For each T in [300 to 700, step 25], examine the column window x ± 3.\n2. Find all black y clusters (markers) in that window.\n3. Classify each marker (circle/star, open/filled).\n4. Assign to curves by tracking (each curve is continuous in T).\n\nThen, for each curve, I have the (T, zT) points and the marker type. Map the marker type to the legend label.\n\nBut which curve is which label? By the vertical ordering of the legend? No — the legend order is just the legend order, not the vertical order in the plot. I need to map by marker type:\n- Open circle curve → EuZn2Sb2\n- Filled circle curve → EuCd2Sb2\n- Open star curve → YbCd2Sb2\n- Filled star curve → YbZn2Sb2\n\nSo as long as I classify each marker's type and each curve has a consistent marker type, I can directly map each curve to its label by its marker type.\n\nSo the plan:\n1. Detect all black markers in the plot (at known x positions, find the y clusters).\n2. Classify each marker (circle/star, open/filled).\