n\nThis is the crux: at low T, the 4 black curves are all bunched together (low zT), so their markers overlap in the column. I can only resolve 2 in the exact center column, but they spread out as I move off-center or as T increases.\n\nOK here's a robust plan. Let me detect ALL markers via 2D template/shape matching over the whole data region (not column-based), get a list of (x, y, shape, fill) markers, then for each of the 4 types collect markers and sort by x to form the series (nearest T).\n\nFor 2D detection, I'll scan candidate centers on a grid and classify each. Let me do: for every pixel (x,y) in data region, check if it's a marker center of a given type. That's expensive but the image is small (405x300).\n\nBetter: find all black pixels, and for marker detection, use the fact that markers are ~7-9 px wide. Let me find \"center\" candidates:\n- For FILLED markers: a pixel (x,y) where a 3x3 (or 5x5) region is mostly black AND it's part of a blob of size 8-20. \n- Actually, let me just find connected components of black in the data region. Each series (markers+line) is one component. Then within each component, find the marker positions.\n\nHmm, but markers of different series might connect if they overlap. At low T they overlap → components merge.\n\nLet me try yet another approach that's very robust: Hough-like template matching.\n\nI'll define 4 normalized binary templates (13x13) for the 4 marker types, extracted from the legend, but I need to remove the line. For the legend, the line is horizontal through the center. I can remove it by... actually the line in the legend is at the marker's vertical center (row cy). For template matching I can just match on the SHAPE which is dominated by the marker body, and the line contributes a horizontal bar. \n\nActually, you know what, let me just carefully do the per-T analysis but resolve merged bands by looking at the 2D structure of each band with a wider x-window, and detect 1-4 markers per band.\n\nLet me reconsider — maybe the cleanest is: for each T, take a window of x = xc-4..xc+4, y = full data range, and look at the 2D black pattern. Then find marker centers by looking for the characteristic patterns.\n\nLet me just dump full 2D patterns for each T with a wide enough window and visually classify. The image is small; let me dump windows of ~11 wide × full height for each T and read them off. I already have good data. Let me be systematic.\n\nLet me reconsider the physics/expected values to sanity check. This is zT vs T for half-Heuslers. The 4 black series:\n- EuZn2Sb2 (open circle)\n- EuCd2Sb2 (filled circle)\n- YbCd2Sb2 (open star)\n- YbZn2Sb2 (filled star)\n\nAnd red EuZn1.8Cd0.2Sb2 (highest at low-mid T), blue YbCd1.6Zn0.4Sb2.\n\nAt T=300, red zT≈0.22 (y≈197), blue zT≈0.20 (y≈200). The black curves at T=300 are around zT 0.15-0.25 (y 195-210). So all 4 black markers at T=300 are in y195-215, overlapping red/blue too. That's why I see a messy blob at x101, y198-221.\n\nOK let me just do 2D template matching properly. I'll extract the 4 templates from the legend (the marker body, accepting some line contamination since the line is thin and horizontal), normalize, and slide over the data region computing match scores. Let me go.\n\nActually, the line in the legend goes through the CENTER of each marker horizontally. So for all 4 templates, the center row has a long horizontal black bar (the line) plus the marker. When I match these against data markers that ALSO have a line (but at a different angle, since data curves are sloped), the match will still be dominated by the marker body. The main discriminative features (top arc vs top point, hollow vs solid center) are preserved.\n\nLet me extract templates as the set of black pixels in a 15x15 window around each legend marker center, centered on the marker. Then for matching, I'll use a smaller core region (say the top 7 rows) to distinguish circle vs star, and center 3x3 to distinguish open vs filled.\n\nSimplest ro