en circle ring just above it + red circle further above; at 450 (right): blue star + open circle ring above it (0.47) + red (0.54); at 475 (far right): open circle between red and blue (0.537).\n\nHmm, so open circle: 400: about 0.37?, 425: about 0.41, 450: about 0.47, 475: 0.537. The increment is 0.04, 0.06, 0.07 — plausible as a smooth curve! And going further back: 375: about 0.33?, 350: about 0.30?, 325: about 0.27?, 300: about 0.24??\n\nBut wait, that would put the open circle near the blue stars (0.306, 0.264, 0.227, 0.194) at 375–300. Hmm, at 300: open circle about 0.24?? That would be above the blue star (0.194)! But in the c300 image at 300 I could only see: red (0.216), blue (0.194), filled circle (0.122), open star (about 0.10), filled star (0.037). I didn't see an open circle around 0.24.\n\nHmm, hmm. Let me look at zz93_c300 again… In that image (x 97–135, y 185–225 → ZT 0.0–0.27): I could see the red circle at the top (0.216), blue star (0.194), then a filled circle + open star pair (about 0.12/0.10), filled star (0.037). I didn't see a ring around 0.24. And ZT 0.24 → cy = 222.7 - 0.24*144.8 = 188 — the top edge of the crop. I don't see it.\n\nSo the open circle at 300 is not 0.24. Then where? Probably hidden under the filled circle (0.122) or the open star (about 0.10).\n\nGiven that at 400: 0.37 (if that's right), 425: 0.41, 450: 0.47, 475: 0.537, 500: 0.601, 525: 0.663, 550: about 0.72, 575: 0.742, 600: 0.806, 625: 0.851, 650: 0.868 — extrapolating back: 375: about 0.32, 350: about 0.29, 325: about 0.26, 300: about 0.23.\n\nBut there's no open circle marker around 0.23–0.26 at 300–350…\n\nAlternatively, maybe the \"open circles\" I see at 425–475 are something else… No, they're clearly open circles (round rings).\n\nWait. Let me recount: maybe the open circle series starts at 425 (10 points: 425–650)? If the series starts at 425 at 0.41, then 450: 0.47, 475: 0.537, 500: 0.601, 525: 0.663, 550: 0.72, 575: 0.742, 600: 0.806, 625: 0.851, 650: 0.868. Th