ly matters for the ground truth: the two series (filled circle = EuCd2Sb2, open star = YbCd2Sb2) in the 300–575 K range. Their true values are probably separated by about 0.01–0.05 in zT. I'll assign:\n\n- Open star (YbCd2Sb2) = upper position in the merged blob (following the trend from the 600+ region, the star is on top).\n- Filled circle (EuCd2Sb2) = lower position.\n\nEstimate from the geometry of the merged blob: for each T, the merged blob spans some y-range. Let me compute: for the merged blob, find (a) the centroid of the eroded core (filled circle), (b) the centroid of the star spikes (open star) = the centroid of black pixels more than 2.5 px away from the core centroid (ring pixels) — though the line will bias this. Or (b) = the topmost spike vertex.\n\nActually, let me try a smarter separation: use the star's top vertex. A star with an up-pointing vertex: the top vertex is at (cx, cy-R), R≈4 (half-height of the star). In the merged blob, the topmost black pixel (excluding the connecting line — the line goes to the upper right, so the topmost pixel in the column at the marker center x is likely the star's top vertex) gives cy_star ≈ ytop + 4.\n\nAnd the filled circle: centroid of the eroded core (2 erosions).\n\nLet me compute both for each overlapping blob and compare. Also, as a cross-check for the direction: at 575, the star should be above the circle; at 300–450 the order is unknown, but probably similar (star on top? or circle on top?).\n\nHmm wait, actually let me reconsider the physics to guess the order:\n- EuZn2Sb2 (open circle) is the topmost black series in the 450–650 range (0.486–0.867).\n- YbCd2Sb2 (open star): 600–700 → 0.63–0.97.\n- EuCd2Sb2 (filled circle): 600–650 → 0.57–0.67.\n- YbZn2Sb2 (filled star): bottommost.\n\nAt low T (300): open star ~0.11–0.13, filled circle ~0.10–0.13 (overlapping), open circle N/A, filled star 0.035.\n\nFrom the visible geometry in the zoom (zz93_m300): the star's spikes are prominent on the right and upper